Entity,iso,Question,Answer,Citation,Pincite_Text,Source_URL,Comment,
African Union,AU,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"12. Sovereignty is an attribute of States. By virtue of sovereignty, States enjoy, under international law, territorial sovereignty, and supremacy in their internal and external affairs. States are also under an obligation to uphold principles of international law including the duty not to infringe on the independence of other States or to violate their territorial sovereignty. 13. The obligation to respect the territorial sovereignty of States is a primary rule that is firmly established in international law, which applies to State conduct in cyberspace.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 2, §II, ¶12; 13 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,This can be reasonably inferred from the AU statement,
African Union,AU,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"14. By virtue of territorial sovereignty, States are entitled to exercise jurisdiction, including legislative, adjudicative, and enforcement authority, over the components of cyberspace that are located on their territory.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 2, §II, ¶14 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a State to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"13. By virtue of territorial sovereignty, States are entitled, within the limits established by the applicable rules of international law, to exercise exclusive control over their land territory and its appurtenances, including the airspace and maritime zones that are subject to the sovereignty of the State. 14. By virtue of territorial sovereignty, States are entitled to exercise jurisdiction, including legislative, adjudicative, and enforcement authority, over the components of cyberspace that are located on their territory.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 2, §II, ¶13; 14 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"18. As a corollary of territorial sovereignty, States shall protect, in accordance with the applicable rules of international law, especially international human rights law and, when applicable, international humanitarian law, natural and legal persons located on their territory against unlawful uses of ICTs in cyberspace that are attributable to foreign States or non-State actors. 16. The African Union further affirms that IHRL requires States to protect the freedom of expression online, including the right to seek, receive, and impart information and ideas and to disseminate opinions through ICTs. Any restrictions imposed by States on these rights must be provided by law and must be limited to what is strictly necessary in a democratic society to respect and protect the rights or reputations of others and to protect national security, public order, public health, or morals... The African Union is of the view that responsible behavior in relation to the use of ICTs in cyberspace requires States to ensure that their conduct does not infringe on the human rights of individuals or peoples in other States.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶16; 18 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"13. The obligation to respect the territorial sovereignty of States is a primary rule that is firmly established in international law, which applies to State conduct in cyberspace.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 2, §II, ¶13 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"16. The African Union affirms that by virtue of territorial sovereignty, any unauthorized access by a State into the ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State is unlawful.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶16 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"16. The African Union affirms that by virtue of territorial sovereignty, any unauthorized access by a State into the ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State is unlawful. Therefore, the African Union emphasizes that the obligation to respect the territorial sovereignty of States, as it applies in cyberspace, does not include a de minimis threshold of harmful effects below which an unauthorized access by a State into the ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State would not be unlawful. The African Union further affirms that cyber operations that are attributable to a State against ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State that causes effects, such as loss or impairment of functionality, on the territory of a third State, may constitute a breach of the territorial sovereignty of that latter State.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶16 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,No,"16. Therefore, the African Union emphasizes that the obligation to respect the territorial sovereignty of States, as it applies in cyberspace, does not include a de minimis threshold of harmful effects below which an unauthorized access by a State into the ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State would not be unlawful. The African Union further affirms that cyber operations that are attributable to a State against ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State that causes effects, such as loss or impairment of functionality, on the territory of a third State, may constitute a breach of the territorial sovereignty of that latter State.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶16 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Yes,"16. The African Union affirms that by virtue of territorial sovereignty, any unauthorized access by a State into the ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State is unlawful. Therefore, the African Union emphasizes that the obligation to respect the territorial sovereignty of States, as it applies in cyberspace, does not include a de minimis threshold of harmful effects below which an unauthorized access by a State into the ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State would not be unlawful.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶16 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"16. The African Union further affirms that cyber operations that are attributable to a State against ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State that causes effects, such as loss or impairment of functionality, on the territory of a third State, may constitute a breach of the territorial sovereignty of that latter State","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶16 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Yes,"16. The African Union affirms that by virtue of territorial sovereignty, any unauthorized access by a State into the ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State is unlawful. Therefore, the African Union emphasizes that the obligation to respect the territorial sovereignty of States, as it applies in cyberspace, does not include a de minimis threshold of harmful effects below which an unauthorized access by a State into the ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State would not be unlawful. The African Union further affirms that cyber operations that are attributable to a State against ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State that causes effects, such as loss or impairment of functionality, on the territory of a third State, may constitute a breach of the territorial sovereignty of that latter State","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶16 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"16. The African Union further affirms that cyber operations that are attributable to a State against ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State that causes effects, such as loss or impairment of functionality, on the territory of a third State, may constitute a breach of the territorial sovereignty of that latter State.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶16 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,The statement does not speak on whether to qualify effects as damage,
African Union,AU,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,No,"16. Therefore, the African Union emphasizes that the obligation to respect the territorial sovereignty of States, as it applies in cyberspace, does not include a de minimis threshold of harmful effects below which an unauthorized access by a State into the ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State would not be unlawful. The African Union further affirms that cyber operations that are attributable to a State against ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State that causes effects, such as loss or impairment of functionality, on the territory of a third State, may constitute a breach of the territorial sovereignty of that latter State.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶16 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,7. Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"16. The African Union further affirms that cyber operations that are attributable to a State against ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State that causes effects, such as loss or impairment of functionality, on the territory of a third State, may constitute a breach of the territorial sovereignty of that latter State.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶16 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,No,"16. The African Union further affirms that cyber operations that are attributable to a State against ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State that causes effects, such as loss or impairment of functionality, on the territory of a third State, may constitute a breach of the territorial sovereignty of that latter State.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶16 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"16. Therefore, the African Union emphasizes that the obligation to respect the territorial sovereignty of States, as it applies in cyberspace, does not include a de minimis threshold of harmful effects below which an unauthorized access by a State into the ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State would not be unlawful. The African Union further affirms that cyber operations that are attributable to a State against ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State that causes effects, such as loss or impairment of functionality, on the territory of a third State, may constitute a breach of the territorial sovereignty of that latter State.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶16 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,See previous comment,
African Union,AU,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"16. Therefore, the African Union emphasizes that the obligation to respect the territorial sovereignty of States, as it applies in cyberspace, does not include a de minimis threshold of harmful effects below which an unauthorized access by a State into the ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State would not be unlawful. The African Union further affirms that cyber operations that are attributable to a State against ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State that causes effects, such as loss or impairment of functionality, on the territory of a third State, may constitute a breach of the territorial sovereignty of that latter State.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶16 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,See previous comment,
African Union,AU,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
African Union,AU,9. Must there be proximate causation between the state cyber operation and the effects or interference prohibited by sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
African Union,AU,9(a) Must the state cyber operation generate prohibited effects directly?,Silent,,,,,
African Union,AU,9(b) Do prohibited effects indirectly generated by a state cyber operation constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
African Union,AU,10. Can state cyber operations that deny access to or degrade data in the control of another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"55. Certain activities undertaken by States, such as the transnational interception of communications, indiscriminate surveillance and data misuse, may constitute a violation of the right to privacy of individuals who are subjected to such conduct, in addition to potentially violating the territorial sovereignty of States on the territory of which such interception occurs.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 9, §VIII, ¶55 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,"If state cyber operations entail data misuse that denies access to or degrades data in control of another state, then they may constitute a violation of sovereignty.",
African Union,AU,10(a) Do state sponsored ransomware operations violate the sovereignty of another State?,Silent,,,,,
African Union,AU,10(b) Can state cyber operations against data residing in cloud servers outside of a State’s territorial jurisdiction constitute a violation of sovereignty that state’s sovereignty?,Silent,,,,,
African Union,AU,10(c) Does the exercise of one State’s law enforcement tools via cyber means in the networks or systems of another State’s territory violate the sovereignty of the latter State?,Yes,"15. The African Union affirms that international law, as it applies to the use of ICTs in cyberspace, does not permit a State to exercise enforcement authority on the territory of a foreign State in response to unlawful cyber activities that emanate from the territory of that foreign State. This applies even if the exercise of such enforcement authority by a State does not have harmful effects, whether virtual or physical, on the territory of a foreign State.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶15 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,"The position paper takes a stand against the use of law enforcement tools via cyber means on the territory of another state even in response to unlawful cyber activities. Although the rule of territorial sovereignty is directly mentioned as being at risk by provisions that allow the exercise of law enforcement tools in the systems of another state, it is not clear whether such activity is a violation.",
African Union,AU,11. Do State cyber operations that interfere at any level with critical infrastructure in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"16. The African Union affirms that by virtue of territorial sovereignty, any unauthorized access by a State into the ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State is unlawful. Therefore, the African Union emphasizes that the obligation to respect the territorial sovereignty of States, as it applies in cyberspace, does not include a de minimis threshold of harmful effects below which an unauthorized access by a State into the ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State would not be unlawful. The African Union further affirms that cyber operations that are attributable to a State against ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State that causes effects, such as loss or impairment of functionality, on the territory of a third State, may constitute a breach of the territorial sovereignty of that latter State.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §II, ¶16 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,"The question isn’t just about unauthorized access but any interference; and since there are ways to interfere without access (e.g., DDoS), we think it’s ambiguous; also the reference to ICT infrastructure is not “critical”? This is ambiguous because although the national position mentions “any unauthorized access” in para 16, it does not specifically use the language of ‘interference.’",
African Union,AU,12. Can State cyber operations that cause significant national economic impacts for another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
African Union,AU,13. Does cyber espionage by one State in or transiting networks or hardware resident in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"55. The African Union is of the view that responsible behavior in relation to the use of ICTs in cyberspace requires States to ensure that their conduct does not infringe on the human rights of individuals or peoples in other States. In particular, certain activities undertaken by States, such as the transnational interception of communications, indiscriminate surveillance and data misuse, may constitute a violation of the right to privacy of individuals who are subjected to such conduct, in addition to potentially violating the territorial sovereignty of States on the territory of which such interception occurs.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 9, §VIII, ¶55 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,"Espionage is discussed in the section on human rights (by reference to interception and surveillance, etc.) but makes reference to such activities “potentially” violating sovereignty.",
African Union,AU,13(a) Can gaining unauthorized access to computer systems or data within a State’s sovereign control violate the rules of sovereignty?,Yes,"14. The jurisdiction of States also applies extraterritorially to ICTs located on aircraft and ships flying the State's flag and satellites and other spacecraft in outer space registered by the State… 16. The African Union affirms that by virtue of territorial sovereignty, any unauthorized access by a State into the ICT infrastructure located on the territory of a foreign State is unlawful.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 2-3, §II, ¶14; 16 (2024).",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,,
African Union,AU,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Silent,,,,,
Australia,AUS,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Silent,,,,,
Australia,AUS,2  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes.,"¶2. To the extent that a State enjoys the right to exercise sovereignty over objects and activities within its territory, it necessarily shoulders corresponding responsibilities to ensure those objects and activities are not used to harm other States.","Australia, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,"Australia’s position on the application of international law to cyberspace, as reiterated in the official compendium, recognizes but does not positively define the extent of sovereign authority over ICT infrastructure. The scope of authority and associated obligations is vague.",
Australia,AUS,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Australia,AUS,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"International human rights law (IHRL) also applies to the use of cyberspace. States have obligations to protect relevant human rights of individuals under their jurisdiction, including the right to privacy, where those rights are exercised or realised through or in cyberspace. Subject to lawful derogations and limitations, states must ensure without distinction individuals’ rights to privacy, freedom of expression and freedom of association online","Supplement to Australia’s Position on the Application of International Law to State Conduct in Cyberspace, Attachment 2",https://perma.cc/LSJ6-GTRW,,
