Austria,AUT,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,¶1. Austria takes the position that respect for state sovereignty is a binding rule of customary international law that also applies to cyber activities.,"Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶1 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,The principle can be reasonably inferred from the Statement,
Austria,AUT,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"States exercise sovereignty over ICT infrastructure on, and persons engaged in cyber activities in, their territory.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, § 3 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,,
Austria,AUT,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a State to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"¶2. State sovereignty entails specific rights – such as a state’s right to exercise exclusive jurisdiction over ICT infrastructure on, and persons engaged in cyber activities in, their territory …","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶2 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,The principle can be reasonably inferred from the Statement,
Austria,AUT,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"¶2. A state’s sovereignty must not be used as a pretext for a state to infringe its human rights obligations, including on data protection and privacy, freedom of expression, and freedom of information.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶2 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,,
Austria,AUT,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"¶1. Austria takes the position that respect for state sovereignty is a binding rule of customary international law that also applies to cyber activities. This means that a cyber activity may violate a state’s sovereignty, even if it does not reach the threshold of, inter alia, an unlawful intervention or threat or use of force. The violation of state sovereignty, through cyber activities of a state or attributable to it, entails the international responsibility of the acting state","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶1 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,,
Austria,AUT,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"¶1. This means that a cyber activity may violate a state’s sovereignty, even if it does not reach the threshold of, inter alia, an unlawful intervention or threat or use of force. The violation of state sovereignty, through cyber activities of a state or attributable to it, entails the international responsibility of the acting state.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶1 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,,
Austria,AUT,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"¶3. A cyber activity violates the sovereignty of another state when it violates a state’s territorial integrity, as well as when it constitutes an interference with or usurpation of an inherently governmental function of a state. Cyber activities that result in physical damage or injury certainly constitute a violation of state sovereignty. Other – only limited – intrusions leading to, e.g. the temporary loss of functionality of critical infrastructure within a state or a cyber activity that temporarily prevents access to essential governmental services, may also constitute a violation of sovereignty. Similarly, cyber espionage activities, including industrial cyber espionage against corporations, within a state’s territory may also violate the state’s sovereignty.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶3 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,,
Austria,AUT,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Silent,,,,,
Austria,AUT,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Austria,AUT,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,¶3. Cyber activities that result in physical damage or injury certainly constitute a violation of state sovereignty.,"Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶3 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,,
Austria,AUT,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Yess,"¶3. Cyber activities that result in physical damage or injury certainly constitute a violation of state sovereignty. Other – only limited – intrusions leading to, e.g. the temporary loss of functionality of critical infrastructure within a state or a cyber activity that temporarily prevents access to essential governmental services, may also constitute a violation of sovereignty. Similarly, cyber espionage activities, including industrial cyber espionage against corporations, within a state’s territory may also violate the state’s sovereignty.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶3 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,"Several examples of physical effects are presented as violations of sovereignty, whereas the mere traversing of data packages without a negative effect is contrasted as a non-violation. It is ambiguous whether certain physical effects may not negatively affect the functioning of ICT infrastructure.",
Austria,AUT,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"¶3. Cyber activities that result in physical damage or injury certainly constitute a violation of state sovereignty. Other – only limited – intrusions leading to, e.g. the temporary loss of functionality of critical infrastructure within a state or a cyber activity that temporarily prevents access to essential governmental services, may also constitute a violation of sovereignty. Similarly, cyber espionage activities, including industrial cyber espionage against corporations, within a state’s territory may also violate the state’s sovereignty.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶3 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,,
Austria,AUT,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,"No mention of repair or replacement of physical components. No definition of damage; only terms of art, such as “substantial material damage” in terms of self-defense.",
Austria,AUT,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,No,"¶3. Cyber activities that result in physical damage or injury certainly constitute a violation of state sovereignty. Other – only limited – intrusions leading to, e.g. the temporary loss of functionality of critical infrastructure within a state or a cyber activity that temporarily prevents access to essential governmental services, may also constitute a violation of sovereignty. Similarly, cyber espionage activities, including industrial cyber espionage against corporations, within a state’s territory may also violate the state’s sovereignty.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶3 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,"Physical damage is not qualified or quantified in terms of sovereignty violations. Moreover, limited intrusions, cyber espionage, and other cyber operations do not necessarily entail any physical damage.",
Austria,AUT,7. Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"Other – only limited – intrusions leading to, e.g. the temporary loss of functionality of critical infrastructure within a state or a cyber activity that temporarily prevents access to essential governmental services, may also constitute a violation of sovereignty.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶3 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,May does not equal will.,
Austria,AUT,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"¶3. Other – only limited – intrusions leading to, e.g. the temporary loss of functionality of critical infrastructure within a state or a cyber activity that temporarily prevents access to essential governmental services, may also constitute a violation of sovereignty.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶3 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,"Sovereignty may be violated by limited intrusions that lead to temporary losses of functionality. The criteria for assessing whether such loss of functionality is a violation, however, are not addressed.",
Austria,AUT,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"¶3. Other – only limited – intrusions leading to, e.g. the temporary loss of functionality of critical infrastructure within a state or a cyber activity that temporarily prevents access to essential governmental services, may also constitute a violation of sovereignty. Example: State A initiates a cyber espionage activity against the Foreign Ministry of state B for the purpose of obtaining illegal access to the internal communication and information system. When state B discovers that its system is compromised, it is required to conduct a total shutdown of the system and temporarily establish an alternative while the IT system is cleaned and restored. Such a cyber activity would constitute a violation of state B’s sovereignty.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶3 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,The criteria for assessing whether any loss of functionality is a violation are not addressed. The example offered suggests that a system shutdown necessitated by cyber espionage activity is a violation of sovereignty.,
Austria,AUT,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"¶3. Other – only limited – intrusions leading to, e.g. the temporary loss of functionality of critical infrastructure within a state or a cyber activity that temporarily prevents access to essential governmental services, may also constitute a violation of sovereignty.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶3 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,The criteria for assessing whether any loss of functionality is a violation are not clear. The example offered (in the row above) suggests that a system shutdown necessitated by cyber espionage activity is a violation of sovereignty.,
Austria,AUT,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶3. A cyber activity violates the sovereignty of another state when it violates a state’s territorial integrity, as well as when it constitutes an interference with or usurpation of an inherently governmental function of a state.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶3 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,,
Austria,AUT,9. Must there be proximate causation between the state cyber operation and the effects or interference prohibited by sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Austria,AUT,9(a) Must the state cyber operation generate prohibited effects directly?,Silent,,,,,
Austria,AUT,9(b) Do prohibited effects indirectly generated by a state cyber operation constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Austria,AUT,10. Can state cyber operations that deny access to or degrade data in the control of another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶9.... a cyber activity that causes the destruction of governmental data in the data embassy of state A in the territory of state B, may be considered a violation of sovereignty of both state A and state B.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 16, §11, ¶9 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,,
Austria,AUT,10(a) Do state sponsored ransomware operations violate the sovereignty of another State?,Silent,,,,,
Austria,AUT,10(b) Can state cyber operations against data residing in cloud servers outside of a State’s territorial jurisdiction constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Austria,AUT,10(c) Does the exercise of one State’s law enforcement tools via cyber means in the networks or systems of another State’s territory violate the sovereignty of the latter State?,Silent,,,,,
Austria,AUT,11. Do State cyber operations that interfere at any level with critical infrastructure in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"¶3. Cyber activities that result in physical damage or injury certainly constitute a violation of state sovereignty. Other – only limited – intrusions leading to, e.g. the temporary loss of functionality of critical infrastructure within a state or a cyber activity that temporarily prevents access to essential governmental services, may also constitute a violation of sovereignty.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶3 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,"While interference with critical infrastructure is discussed as a potential violation of sovereignty, there is no mention of what should be the amount/level of such interference.",
Austria,AUT,12. Can State cyber operations that cause significant national economic impacts for another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Austria,AUT,13. Does cyber espionage by one State in or transiting networks or hardware resident in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"¶3. Similarly, cyber espionage activities, including industrial cyber espionage against corporations, within a state’s territory may also violate the state’s sovereignty.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶3 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,“may” It is ambiguous because the language uses the word “may.”,
Austria,AUT,13(a) Can gaining unauthorized access to computer systems or data within a State’s sovereign control violate the rules of sovereignty,Yes,"¶3. A cyber activity violates the sovereignty of another state when it violates a state’s territorial integrity, as well as when it constitutes an interference with or usurpation of an inherently governmental function of a state.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 4, §3, ¶3 (2024).",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,,
Austria,AUT,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Silent,,,,,
Belgium,BEL,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"¶1. Belgium considers the principle of sovereignty to be a primary rule of international law, the violation of which by another State engages the international responsibility of the latter. The principle of sovereignty was defined in the Island of Palmas arbitral award of 1928, which states: “Sovereignty in the relations between States signifies independence. Independence in regard to a portion of the globe is the right to exercise therein, to the exclusion of any other State, the functions of a State .” Two elements emerge from this definition: the territorial aspect and state functions.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,,
Belgium,BEL,2.  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"¶1. The principle of sovereignty was defined in the Island of Palmas arbitral award of 1928, which states: “Sovereignty in the relations between States signifies independence. Independence in regard to a portion of the globe is the right to exercise therein, to the exclusion of any other State, the functions of a State.” Two elements emerge from this definition: the territorial aspect and state functions. ¶2. The sovereignty of States can thus be violated by cyber operations carried out by another State if they cause a certain degree of damage to their territorial integrity. There is no doubt that a State's sovereignty can be violated if cyber operations cause physical damage to infrastructure, injury to people and/or a loss of system functionality.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,Belgium admits that sovereignty involves a territorial aspect that grants a state the exclusive right to exercise rights over its own territory independently.,
Belgium,BEL,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a State to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Belgium,BEL,2(b) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a State to control government data stored or transmitted inside its territory?,Silent,,,,,
Belgium,BEL,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"¶1. The obligations of international and regional human rights conventions to which Belgium is a party must also be respected in the context of operations in cyberspace.  ¶2. Certain fundamental rights, such as the right to privacy, freedom of expression and access to information, are particularly at risk in cyberspace. However, these rights must be respected and protected both online and offline.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,,
Belgium,BEL,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"¶1. Firstly, a cyber operation could be considered as a violation of the principle of sovereignty. Belgium considers the principle of sovereignty to be a primary rule of international law, the violation of which by another State engages the international responsibility of the latter.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,,
Belgium,BEL,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"¶1. Firstly, a cyber operation could be considered as a violation of the principle of sovereignty. Belgium considers the principle of sovereignty to be a primary rule of international law, the violation of which by another State engages the international responsibility of the latter.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,,
Belgium,BEL,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"¶2. The sovereignty of States can thus be violated by cyber operations carried out by another State if they cause a certain degree of damage to their territorial integrity. There is no doubt that a State's sovereignty can be violated if cyber operations cause physical damage to infrastructure, injury to people and/or a loss of system functionality.  ¶3. It is not excluded that a cyber operation that takes place on the territory of another State and does not cause loss of functionality or physical damage may nevertheless violate the sovereignty of a state.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,,
Belgium,BEL,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Ambiguous,"¶2. The sovereignty of States can thus be violated by cyber operations carried out by another State if they cause a certain degree of damage to their territorial integrity. There is no doubt that a State's sovereignty can be violated if cyber operations cause physical damage to infrastructure, injury to people and/or a loss of system functionality.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,,
Belgium,BEL,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Belgium,BEL,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶2. The sovereignty of States can thus be violated by cyber operations carried out by another State if they cause a certain degree of damage to their territorial integrity. There is no doubt that a State's sovereignty can be violated if cyber operations cause physical damage to infrastructure, injury to people and/or a loss of system functionality.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,,
Belgium,BEL,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Ambiguous,"¶2. The sovereignty of States can thus be violated by cyber operations carried out by another State if they cause a certain degree of damage to their territorial integrity. There is no doubt that a State's sovereignty can be violated if cyber operations cause physical damage to infrastructure, injury to people and/or a loss of system functionality.  ¶3. It is not excluded that a cyber operation that takes place on the territory of another State and does not cause loss of functionality or physical damage may nevertheless violate the sovereignty of a state.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,"Belgium does not rule out cyber operations that do not cause physical damage on a state’s territory as violations of sovereignty, although the analysis usually involves assessing a certain degree of damage to territorial integrity or interference with state functions.",
Belgium,BEL,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"¶2. The sovereignty of States can thus be violated by cyber operations carried out by another State if they cause a certain degree of damage to their territorial integrity. There is no doubt that a State's sovereignty can be violated if cyber operations cause physical damage to infrastructure, injury to people and/or a loss of system functionality.  ¶3. It is not excluded that a cyber operation that takes place on the territory of another State and does not cause loss of functionality or physical damage may nevertheless violate the sovereignty of a state.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,"Belgium does not rule out cyber operations that do not cause physical damage to tangible things or injury to persons as violations of sovereignty, although the analysis usually involves assessing a certain degree of damage to territorial integrity or interference with state functions.",
Belgium,BEL,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,,
Belgium,BEL,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,No,"¶2. The sovereignty of States can thus be violated by cyber operations carried out by another State if they cause a certain degree of damage to their territorial integrity. There is no doubt that a State's sovereignty can be violated if cyber operations cause physical damage to infrastructure, injury to people and/or a loss of system functionality.  ¶3. It is not excluded that a cyber operation that takes place on the territory of another State and does not cause loss of functionality or physical damage may nevertheless violate the sovereignty of a state.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,"Although Belgium does not characterize or quantify the certain degree of damage to territorial integrity required for a sovereignty violation, non-physical effects are not ruled out.",
Belgium,BEL,7 Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶2. The sovereignty of States can thus be violated by cyber operations carried out by another State if they cause a certain degree of damage to their territorial integrity. There is no doubt that a State's sovereignty can be violated if cyber operations cause physical damage to infrastructure, injury to people and/or a loss of system functionality.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,,
Belgium,BEL,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Yes,"¶2. The sovereignty of States can thus be violated by cyber operations carried out by another State if they cause a certain degree of damage to their territorial integrity. There is no doubt that a State's sovereignty can be violated if cyber operations cause physical damage to infrastructure, injury to people and/or a loss of system functionality.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,,
Belgium,BEL,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Yes,"¶2. The sovereignty of States can thus be violated by cyber operations carried out by another State if they cause a certain degree of damage to their territorial integrity. There is no doubt that a State's sovereignty can be violated if cyber operations cause physical damage to infrastructure, injury to people and/or a loss of system functionality.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,,
Belgium,BEL,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Yes,"¶2. The sovereignty of States can thus be violated by cyber operations carried out by another State if they cause a certain degree of damage to their territorial integrity. There is no doubt that a State's sovereignty can be violated if cyber operations cause physical damage to infrastructure, injury to people and/or a loss of system functionality.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,,
Belgium,BEL,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶2. The sovereignty of States can thus be violated by cyber operations carried out by another State if they cause a certain degree of damage to their territorial integrity. There is no doubt that a State's sovereignty can be violated if cyber operations cause physical damage to infrastructure, injury to people and/or a loss of system functionality.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,Belgium sets a low bar for finding a sovereignty violation based on a cyber operation that interferences with state functions.,
