Brazil,BRA,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,¶1. State sovereignty is one of the founding principles of international law.,"UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 18, §1, ¶1, (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Brazil,BRA,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a State to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"¶1. [State sovereignty] is applicable as a standalone rule, including to the use of ICTs by States, and entails an independent obligation of “every State to respect the territorial sovereignty of others”.","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 18, §1, ¶1, (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Brazil,BRA,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"¶1. [State sovereignty] is applicable as a standalone rule, including to the use of ICTs by States, and entails an independent obligation of “every State to respect the territorial sovereignty of others”. Currently, there is neither broad state practice nor sufficient opinio juris to generate new customary international norm allowing for the violation of State sovereignty, including by means of ICTs. ¶2. Violations of State sovereignty by another State, including by means of ICTs, constitute an internationally wrongful act and entail the international responsibility of the State in violation. Interceptions of telecommunications, for instance, whether or not they are considered to have crossed the threshold of an intervention in the internal affairs of another State, would nevertheless be considered an internationally wrongful act because they violate state sovereignty.","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 18, §1, ¶1-2, (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Brazil,BRA,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,No,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,SILENT,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"¶2. Similarly, cyber operations against information systems located in another State’s territory or causing extraterritorial effects might also constitute a breach of sovereignty.","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 18, §1, ¶2, (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,Brazil’s position broadly defines cyber operations causing extraterritorial effects as a potential breach of sovereignty. Extraterritorial effects may ambiguously entail physical effects.,
Brazil,BRA,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"¶2. Violations of State sovereignty by another State, including by means of ICTs, constitute an internationally wrongful act and entail the international responsibility of the State in violation. Interceptions of telecommunications, for instance, whether or not they are considered to have crossed the threshold of an intervention in the internal affairs of another State, would nevertheless be considered an internationally wrongful act because they violate state sovereignty. Similarly, cyber operations against information systems located in another State’s territory or causing extraterritorial effects might also constitute a breach of sovereignty.","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 18, §1, ¶2, (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Brazil,BRA,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,7 Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,9. Must there be proximate causation between the state cyber operation and the effects or interference prohibited by sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,9(a) Must the state cyber operation generate prohibited effects directly?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,9(b) Do prohibited effects indirectly generated by a state cyber operation constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,10. Can state cyber operations that deny access to or degrade data in the control of another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,10(a) Do state sponsored ransomware operations violate the sovereignty of another State?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,10(b) Do state cyber operations against data residing in cloud servers outside of a State’s territorial jurisdiction constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,10(c) Does the exercise of one State’s law enforcement tools via cyber means in the networks or systems of another State’s territory violate the sovereignty of the latter State?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,11. Do State cyber operations that interfere at any level with critical infrastructure in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,12. Can State cyber operations that cause significant national economic impacts for another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Brazil,BRA,13. Does cyber espionage by one State in or transiting networks or hardware resident in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶2. Interceptions of telecommunications, for instance, whether or not they are considered to have crossed the threshold of an intervention in the internal affairs of another State, would nevertheless be considered an internationally wrongful act because they violate state sovereignty. Similarly, cyber operations against information systems located in another State’s territory or causing extraterritorial effects might also constitute a breach of sovereignty.","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 18, §1, ¶2, (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Brazil,BRA,13(a) Can gaining unauthorized access to computer systems or data within a State’s sovereign control violate the rules of sovereignty?,Yes,"¶2. Interceptions of telecommunications, for instance, whether or not they are considered to have crossed the threshold of an intervention in the internal affairs of another State, would nevertheless be considered an internationally wrongful act because they violate state sovereignty. Similarly, cyber operations against information systems located in another State’s territory or causing extraterritorial effects might also constitute a breach of sovereignty.","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 18, §1, ¶2, (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,This is a reasonable inference from the breadth of the Statement,
Brazil,BRA,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Silent,,,,,
Canada,CAN,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"¶10. Sovereignty is a fundamental element of international law and international relations. It is axiomatic that the principle of sovereignty applies in cyberspace, just as it does elsewhere. It animates a number of obligations for all States.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶10 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,,
Canada,CAN,2.  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"¶13. Territorial sovereignty is a rule under international law. . . . States enjoy sovereignty over their territory, including in particular infrastructure located within their territory and activities associated with that infrastructure. ...","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶13 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,This can be reasonably inferred from Canada’s statement.,
Canada,CAN,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a 
State to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"¶12. This concept is also reflected in Canadian jurisprudence where Canada’s highest court found that “sovereignty” referred to “the various powers, rights and duties that accompany statehood under international law…” ¶13. Territorial sovereignty is a rule under international law. Every State must respect the territorial sovereignty of every other State. States enjoy sovereignty over their territory, including in particular infrastructure located within their territory and activities associated with that infrastructure...","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶12-13 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,This can be reasonably inferred from Canada’s statement.,
Canada,CAN,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"¶8. Canada affirms that international law applies to the activities of every State in cyberspace. This includes the United Nations Charter (UN Charter) in its entirety and customary international law... Canada further recognizes the obligations arising, in a non-exhaustive manner, from international human rights law (IHRL), international humanitarian law (IHL) and in relation to the law of State responsibility. ¶39. Canada notes that according to Article 2(1) of The International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, each State Party is required to respect and to ensure to all individuals within its territory and subject to its jurisdiction the rights recognized in that instrument, without distinction. ¶40. The internationally recognized human rights that are of particular concern in relation to cyberspace include the right to freedom of expression and to hold opinions without unlawful interference, freedom of association and of peaceful assembly, freedom from discrimination, and the right not to be subjected to arbitrary or unlawful interference with one’s privacy or correspondence.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶8; 39 -40 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,This can be reasonably inferred from Canada’s statement.,
Canada,CAN,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"¶13. Territorial sovereignty is a rule under international law. Every State must respect the territorial sovereignty of every other State... An infringement upon the affected State’s territorial integrity, or an interference with or usurpation of inherently governmental functions of the affected State, would be a violation of territorial sovereignty.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶13 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,,
Canada,CAN,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"¶17. The rule of territorial sovereignty does not require consent for every cyber activity that has effects, including some loss of functionality, in another State. Activities causing negligible or de minimis effects would not constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty regardless of whether they are conducted in the cyber or non-cyber context. Nor are States precluded by the rule of territorial sovereignty from taking measures that have negligible or de minimis effects to defend against the harmful activity of malicious cyber actors or to protect their national security interests. For example, Canada considers that a cyber activity that requires rebooting or the reinstallation of an operating system is likely not a violation of territorial sovereignty.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶17 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,This can be reasonably inferred from Canada’s statement and the examples offered for sovereignty violations in contrast to the elements proferred for non-intervention,
Canada,CAN,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"¶14. In assessing the possible infringement of a State’s territorial sovereignty, several key factors must be considered. The scope, scale, impact or severity of disruption caused, including the disruption of economic and societal activities, essential services, inherently governmental functions, public order or public safety must be assessed to determine whether a violation of the territorial sovereignty of the affected State has taken place.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶14-15;17 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,,
Canada,CAN,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Yes,"¶15. Cyber activities that rise above a level of negligible or de minimis effects, causing significant harmful effects within the territory of another State without that State’s consent, could amount to a violation of the rule of territorial sovereignty with respect to the affected State. . . .  ¶17. The rule of territorial sovereignty does not require consent for every cyber activity that has effects, including some loss of functionality, in another State. Activities causing negligible or de minimis effects would not constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty regardless of whether they are conducted in the cyber or non-cyber context. Nor are States precluded by the rule of territorial sovereignty from taking measures that have negligible or de minimis effects to defend against the harmful activity of malicious cyber actors or to protect their national security interests. For example, Canada considers that a cyber activity that requires rebooting or the reinstallation of an operating system is likely not a violation of territorial sovereignty.",,,,
Canada,CAN,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Canada,CAN,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶14. In assessing the possible infringement of a State’s territorial sovereignty, several key factors must be considered. The scope, scale, impact or severity of disruption caused, including the disruption of economic and societal activities, essential services, inherently governmental functions, public order or public safety must be assessed to determine whether a violation of the territorial sovereignty of the affected State has taken place. ¶15. In general, the impact or severity of cyber effects will be evaluated in the same manner and according to the same criteria as for physical activities.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶14-15 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,This can be inferred from the Statement,
Canada,CAN,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,No,"¶15. In general, the impact or severity of cyber effects will be evaluated in the same manner and according to the same criteria as for physical activities. Cyber activities that rise above a level of negligible or de minimis effects, causing significant harmful effects within the territory of another State without that State’s consent, could amount to a violation of the rule of territorial sovereignty with respect to the affected State.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶15 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,Canada specifically recognizes a de minimus rule, notes only that significantly harmful physical effects produced by cyber operations without the consent of the victim state may constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty
Canada,CAN,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"¶16. Cyber activities that cause a loss of functionality with respect to cyber infrastructure located within the territory of the affected State may also constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty if the resulting loss of functionality causes significant harmful effects similar to those caused by physical damage to persons or property. ¶18. The other key basis for assessing a violation of territorial sovereignty is whether a cyber activity interferes with or usurps the inherently governmental functions of another State. Cyber activities that have significant harmful effects on the exercise of inherently governmental functions would constitute an internationally wrongful act. For Canada, this would include government activities in areas such as health care services, law enforcement, administration of elections, tax collection, national defence and the conduct of international relations, and the services on which these depend. There can be a violation of territorial sovereignty by way of effects on governmental functions regardless of whether there is physical damage, injury, or loss of functionality.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶16; 18 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,,
Canada,CAN,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Ambiguous,"¶16. Cyber activities that cause a loss of functionality with respect to cyber infrastructure located within the territory of the affected State may also constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty if the resulting loss of functionality causes significant harmful effects similar to those caused by physical damage to persons or property. For example, a violation of the territorial sovereignty will occur when the cyber activity creates a significant harmful effect that necessitates the repair or replacement of physical components of cyber infrastructure in the affected State.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶16 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,,
Canada,CAN,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,No,"¶16. In general, the impact or severity of cyber effects will be evaluated in the same manner and according to the same criteria as for physical activities. Cyber activities that rise above a level of negligible or de minimis effects, causing significant harmful effects within the territory of another State without that State’s consent, could amount to a violation of the rule of territorial sovereignty with respect to the affected State. ¶18. The other key basis for assessing a violation of territorial sovereignty is whether a cyber activity interferes with or usurps the inherently governmental functions of another State... There can be a violation of territorial sovereignty by way of effects on governmental functions regardless of whether there is physical damage, injury, or loss of functionality. An example would be a cyber activity that interrupts health care delivery by blocking access to patient health records or emergency room services, resulting in risk to the health or life of patients.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶16; 18 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,,
Canada,CAN,7 Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,¶16. Cyber activities that cause a loss of functionality with respect to cyber infrastructure located within the territory of the affected State may also constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty if the resulting loss of functionality causes significant harmful effects similar to those caused by physical damage to persons or property.,"Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶16 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,"Canada’s position acknowledges that cyber operations affecting cyber infrastructure, causing a loss of functionality, and leading to significant harmful effects within the territory of the targeted state may constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty. The qualification of “significant harmful effects” clarifies the impact and severity requisite of a cyber activity that rises to the level of a violation.",
Canada,CAN,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,No,"¶16. Cyber activities that cause a loss of functionality with respect to cyber infrastructure located within the territory of the affected State may also constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty if the resulting loss of functionality causes significant harmful effects similar to those caused by physical damage to persons or property. ¶17. The rule of territorial sovereignty does not require consent for every cyber activity that has effects, including some loss of functionality, in another State. Activities causing negligible or de minimis effects would not constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty regardless of whether they are conducted in the cyber or non-cyber context. Nor are States precluded by the rule of territorial sovereignty from taking measures that have negligible or de minimis effects to defend against the harmful activity of malicious cyber actors or to protect their national security interests. For example, Canada considers that a cyber activity that requires rebooting or the reinstallation of an operating system is likely not a violation of territorial sovereignty.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶16, 17 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,Must cause harmful effects somewhere above a de minimis line.,
Canada,CAN,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"¶16. Cyber activities that cause a loss of functionality with respect to cyber infrastructure located within the territory of the affected State may also constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty if the resulting loss of functionality causes significant harmful effects similar to those caused by physical damage to persons or property. ¶17. The rule of territorial sovereignty does not require consent for every cyber activity that has effects, including some loss of functionality, in another State. Activities causing negligible or de minimis effects would not constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty regardless of whether they are conducted in the cyber or non-cyber context. Nor are States precluded by the rule of territorial sovereignty from taking measures that have negligible or de minimis effects to defend against the harmful activity of malicious cyber actors or to protect their national security interests. For example, Canada considers that a cyber activity that requires rebooting or the reinstallation of an operating system is likely not a violation of territorial sovereignty.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶16, 17 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,It can also read as ambiguous because the decision is conditional upon “if the resulting loss of functionality causes significant harmful effects similar to those caused by physical damage to persons or property”,
Canada,CAN,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,¶20. It is possible that a series of cyber activities could lead to significant harmful effects that violate the rule of territorial sovereignty. This is the case even if the individual cyber activity on its own would not reach this threshold.,"Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶20 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,Canada’s position admits of the possibility that effects that individually would not amount to a violation could amount to a violation based on the aggregate effect across systems.,
Canada,CAN,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶18. The other key basis for assessing a violation of territorial sovereignty is whether a cyber activity interferes with or usurps the inherently governmental functions of another State. Cyber activities that have significant harmful effects on the exercise of inherently governmental functions would constitute an internationally wrongful act. For Canada, this would include government activities in areas such as health care services, law enforcement, administration of elections, tax collection, national defence and the conduct of international relations, and the services on which these depend. There can be a violation of territorial sovereignty by way of effects on governmental functions regardless of whether there is physical damage, injury, or loss of functionality. An example would be a cyber activity that interrupts health care delivery by blocking access to patient health records or emergency room services, resulting in risk to the health or life of patients.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶18 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,,
Canada,CAN,9. Must there be proximate causation between the state cyber operation and the effects or interference prohibited by sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Canada,CAN,9(a) Must the state cyber operation generate prohibited effects directly?,Silent,,,,,
Canada,CAN,9(b) Do prohibited effects indirectly generated by a state cyber operation constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Canada,CAN,10. Can state cyber operations that deny access to or degrade data in the control of another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶18. There can be a violation of territorial sovereignty by way of effects on governmental functions regardless of whether there is physical damage, injury, or loss of functionality. An example would be a cyber activity that interrupts health care delivery by blocking access to patient health records or emergency room services, resulting in risk to the health or life of patients.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶18 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,,
Canada,CAN,10(a) Do state sponsored ransomware operations violate the sovereignty of another State?,Silent,,,,,
Canada,CAN,10(b) Can state cyber operations against data residing in cloud servers outside of a State’s territorial jurisdiction constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Canada,CAN,10(c) Does the exercise of one State’s law enforcement tools via cyber means in the networks or systems of another State’s territory violate the sovereignty of the latter State?,Ambiguous,"¶15. It is also important to note that cyber activities with effects in another State do not constitute physical presence in the territory of that State. As such, territorial sovereignty is not violated by virtue merely of remote activities having been carried out on or through the cyber infrastructure located within the territory of another State.  Furthermore, cyber activities carried out remotely from within Canada with negligible effects in a foreign State do not involve an extraterritorial exercise of enforcement jurisdiction by Canada.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶15 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,"Canada’s position  focuses on the effects of a cyber operation, not the purpose, specifically noting that operations with negligible effects do not amount to the exercise of enforcement jurisdiction.",
Canada,CAN,11. Do State cyber operations that interfere at any level with critical infrastructure in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,No,"¶17. Activities causing negligible or de minimis effects would not constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty regardless of whether they are conducted in the cyber or non-cyber context. Nor are States precluded by the rule of territorial sovereignty from taking measures that have negligible or de minimis effects to defend against the harmful activity of malicious cyber actors or to protect their national security interests. For example, Canada considers that a cyber activity that requires rebooting or the reinstallation of an operating system is likely not a violation of territorial sovereignty. ¶18. There can be a violation of territorial sovereignty by way of effects on governmental functions regardless of whether there is physical damage, injury, or loss of functionality. An example would be a cyber activity that interrupts health care delivery by blocking access to patient health records or emergency room services, resulting in risk to the health or life of patients.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶17, 18 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,"Canada recognizes a de minimis rule, which it does not limit to effects against any particular systems or functional areas.",
Canada,CAN,12. Can State cyber operations that cause significant national economic impacts for another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶14. In assessing the possible infringement of a State’s territorial sovereignty, several key factors must be considered. The scope, scale, impact or severity of disruption caused, including the disruption of economic and societal activities, essential services, inherently governmental functions, public order or public safety must be assessed to determine whether a violation of the territorial sovereignty of the affected State has taken place.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶14 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,,
Canada,CAN,13. Does cyber espionage by one State in or transiting networks or hardware resident in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,No,"¶19. Importantly, some cyber activities, such as cyber espionage, do not amount to a breach of territorial sovereignty, and hence to a violation of international law.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶19 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,,
Canada,CAN,13(a) Can gaining unauthorized access to computer systems or data within a State’s sovereign control violate the rules of sovereignty?,Ambiguous,"¶19. Importantly, some cyber activities, such as cyber espionage, do not amount to a breach of territorial sovereignty, and hence to a violation of international law.","Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶19 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,,
Canada,CAN,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Yes,¶21. Canada will assess whether a violation of territorial sovereignty has occurred on a case-by-case basis.,"Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace, ¶21 (2022).",https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,,
Chile,CHL,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"¶5. Likewise, based on the principle of sovereignty, every state has an obligation to respect the territorial integrity and independence of other states and must faithfully discharge its international obligations, including as regards the principle of nonintervention",OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Chile,/static/documents/40_oas-inter-american-juridical-committee-questionnaire---respo.pdf,"There is no specific mention of it, but it can be inferred.",
Chile,CHL,2.  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"¶1. The State of Chile, through its National Cyber Defense Policy, has declared that it will protect its critical information infrastructure by exercising its sovereignty over those networks and digital resources. ¶2. Cyber operations occur in a particular territory and involve objects, or are carried out by persons or entities over which the state may exercise sovereignty. The State of Chile has sovereignty over a cyber or information infrastructure located in its territory and over activities associated with that infrastructure.",OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Chile,/static/documents/21_chile-oas-inter-american-juridical-committee-questionnaire.pdf,,
Chile,CHL,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a State to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"¶1. The State of Chile, through its National Cyber Defense Policy, has declared that it will protect its critical information infrastructure by exercising its sovereignty over those networks and digital resources. ¶2. The State of Chile has sovereignty over a cyber or information infrastructure located in its territory and over activities associated with that infrastructure.",OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Chile,/static/documents/21_chile-oas-inter-american-juridical-committee-questionnaire.pdf,,
Chile,CHL,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"¶3. Accordingly, a state is free to take any step its considers necessary or appropriate with respect to its cyber infrastructure as well as in relation to cyber operations that have taken place on that infrastructure and with respect to individuals involved in them, as long as those measures do not violate the rule of international law – for example, those contained in international human rights law or international humanitarian law.",OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Chile,/static/documents/21_chile-oas-inter-american-juridical-committee-questionnaire.pdf,,
Chile,CHL,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Ambiguous,"¶4. Hence, every time a state considers carrying out a cyber-operation, it should consider ensuring it does not affect the sovereignty of another. . . . Likewise, based on the principle of sovereignty, every state has an obligation to respect the territorial integrity and independence of other states and must faithfully discharge its international obligations, including as regards the principle of nonintervention. Cyber operations that hinder another state from exercising its sovereignty therefore constitute a violation of that sovereignty and are prohibited under international law.",OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Chile,/static/documents/21_chile-oas-inter-american-juridical-committee-questionnaire.pdf,,
Chile,CHL,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Ambiguous,"¶4. Hence, every time a state considers carrying out a cyber-operation, it should consider ensuring it does not affect the sovereignty of another. . . . Likewise, based on the principle of sovereignty, every state has an obligation to respect the territorial integrity and independence of other states and must faithfully discharge its international obligations, including as regards the principle of nonintervention. Cyber operations that hinder another state from exercising its sovereignty therefore constitute a violation of that sovereignty and are prohibited under international law.",OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Chile,/static/documents/21_chile-oas-inter-american-juridical-committee-questionnaire.pdf,,
Chile,CHL,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,No,,,,,
