Colombia,COL,Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Ambiguous,"¶1. In Colombia, the concept of sovereignty, understood as independence, is a foundational element of its foreign relations, as outlined in the Political Constitution. Colombia understands sovereignty as encompassing both internal and external dimensions and conferring rights and imposing duties on States under International Law ¶4. Sovereignty and related applicable international rules and principles are applicable to the conduct of ICT-related activities by States and to their jurisdiction over ICTs infrastructure within their territory. ¶6. Colombia recognizes the special characteristics of cyberspace … [present] a significant challenge, that involves striking a delicate balance between national sovereignty and the need for cooperation and adherence to international norms … in the cyber domain, where national and global interests often come into conflict … States need to further develop their legal views on how sovereignty applies in the cyber context, particularly in instances involving multiple jurisdictions at play and where the territorial component is absent or complex.”","Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pgs. 7-8, §3, ¶1, 4, 6 (2025)",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,,
Colombia,COL,Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"¶4. Sovereignty and related applicable international rules and principles are applicable to the conduct of ICT-related activities by States and to their jurisdiction over ICTs infrastructure within their territory.  ¶5. Thus, States exercise jurisdiction over ICTs infrastructure within their territory by, inter alia, establishing policies and laws as inherent functions of a State, as well as the necessary mechanisms to protect ICTs infrastructure within their territory from ICT-related threats.","Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 8, § 3, ¶4-5 (2025).",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,,
Colombia,COL,"Does sovereignty encompass the right of a State to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"¶4. Sovereignty and related applicable international rules and principles are applicable to the conduct of ICT-related activities by States and to their jurisdiction over ICTs infrastructure within their territory.  ¶5. Thus, States exercise jurisdiction over ICTs infrastructure within their territory by, inter alia, establishing policies and laws as inherent functions of a State, as well as the necessary mechanisms to protect ICTs infrastructure within their territory from ICT-related threats.","Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 8, § 3, ¶4-5 (2025).",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,,
Colombia,COL,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"¶1. International Human Rights Law applies to activities in cyberspace. State’s initiatives established for the purpose of guaranteeing the security of information and communication technologies, as well as all actions in cyberspace, must respect human rights and the fundamental freedoms set forth in international instruments, such as the Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, the Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights, the American Convention on Human Rights, and the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, and in Customary International Law.","Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 10, § 7, ¶1  (2025).",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,,
Colombia,COL,"Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Ambiguous,"¶1. In Colombia, the concept of sovereignty, understood as independence, is a foundational element of its foreign relations, as outlined in the Political Constitution. Colombia understands sovereignty as encompassing both internal and external dimensions and conferring rights and imposing duties on States under International Law.","Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 7, §3, ¶1 (2025).",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,,
Colombia,COL,Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Ambiguous,"¶2. In this regard, Colombia acknowledges that sovereignty entails both rights and duties and is susceptible to violations that may constitute international wrongful acts”","Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 7, §3, ¶2 (2025).",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,,
