Costa Rica,CRI,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"¶18. Sovereignty is a fundamental principle of international law, underpinning the entire international legal order and firmly grounding the position of States therein.",Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,,
Costa Rica,CRI,2.  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"¶18. ...Sovereignty has been traditionally understood in a territorial and physical sense. It means, first and foremost, a State’s right to exercise legislative, adjudicative, and enforcement jurisdiction in its territory, as well as the power to regulate the conduct of certain persons and events abroad... ¶19. Sovereignty also applies to cyberspace, including its physical and non-physical components. After all, in the digital age, a State’s sovereign powers over its territory and other objects or subjects are increasingly exercised through and dependent on the use of ICTs.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,,
Costa Rica,CRI,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a State to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"¶18. ...Sovereignty has been traditionally understood in a territorial and physical sense. It means, first and foremost, a State’s right to exercise legislative, adjudicative, and enforcement jurisdiction in its territory, as well as the power to regulate the conduct of certain persons and events abroad. Sovereign rights also have corollary duties, in particular, the obligation of a State to respect other States’ sovereign rights and protect them within its territory. ¶19. Sovereignty also applies to cyberspace, including its physical and non-physical components. After all, in the digital age, a State’s sovereign powers over its territory and other objects or subjects are increasingly exercised through and dependent on the use of ICTs.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,The answer can be inferred from the Statement.,
Costa Rica,CRI,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"¶31. As affirmed by the UN Human Rights Council, human rights apply online just as they do offline. States have obligations to respect, protect and ensure the enjoyment of a range of human rights, including civil and political rights as well as social, economic and cultural rights. ¶34. Costa Rica stresses that the test for assessing the lawfulness of such limitations generally requires States to assess whether the limitation is grounded in sufficiently clear and accessible laws (legality), fulfils a legitimate purpose (legitimacy), and is necessary and proportionate to achieve this aim (necessity and  proportionality). This test must always be applied when the application of the rights and obligations discussed above implicates human rights online.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,,
Costa Rica,CRI,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"¶19. Sovereignty also applies to cyberspace, including its physical and non-physical components. After all, in the digital age, a State’s sovereign powers over its territory and other objects or subjects are increasingly exercised through and dependent on the use of ICTs. In Costa Rica’s view, sovereignty is also a self-standing right accompanied by a binding international legal obligation that can be breached by both cyber and noncyber activities.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,,
Costa Rica,CRI,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"¶19. Sovereignty also applies to cyberspace, including its physical and non-physical components. After all, in the digital age, a State’s sovereign powers over its territory and other objects or subjects are increasingly exercised through and dependent on the use of ICTs. In Costa Rica’s view, sovereignty is also a self-standing right accompanied by a binding international legal obligation that can be breached by both cyber and non- cyber activities.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,,
Costa Rica,CRI,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"¶20. Such breaches may occur when cyber operations cause physical damage or loss of functionality of cyber infrastructure located in the victim State, regardless of ownership... For Costa Rica, a loss of functionality of these devices may occur in two ways... First, when the cyber operation attributable to another State entails the need to repair or replace physical components of the targeted cyber infrastructure or compromises physical equipment reliant on such infrastructure. Second, loss of functionality may occur if the operating system or database upon which the targeted cyber infrastructure relies stops functioning as intended, as may be the case, for instance, as a result of ransomware.  ¶21. Breaches of sovereignty may also occur when a State engages in cyber operations that constitute a usurpation of inherently governmental functions, irrespective of any physical or non-physical effects on hardware or software located in the territory of the victim State.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,,
Costa Rica,CRI,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Silent,,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶19. In Costa Rica’s view, sovereignty is also a self-standing right accompanied by a binding international legal obligation that can be breached by both cyber and noncyber activities.  ¶20. Such breaches may occur when cyber operations cause physical damage or loss of functionality of cyber infrastructure located in the victim State, regardless of ownership.",Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,Costa Rica notes that physical effects “may” amount to a breach.  It does not specify that any physical effects will constitute a breach.,
Costa Rica,CRI,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Ambiguous,"¶20. Such breaches may occur when cyber operations cause physical damage or loss of functionality of cyber infrastructure located in the victim State, regardless of ownership. Examples range from personal computers to programmable logic controllers or industrial computers that control energy, water, and sanitation facilities. For Costa Rica, a loss of functionality of these devices may occur in two ways...","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,"Costa Rica provides 2 ways to identify a loss of functionality and several examples of physical damage. This may mean, “any” physical effects will not constitute a violation",
Costa Rica,CRI,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"¶20. Breaches of sovereignty may also occur when a State engages in cyber operations that constitute a usurpation of inherently governmental functions, irrespective of any physical or non-physical effects on hardware or software located in the territory of the victim State. Examples of cyber operations amounting to this type of violation are those interfering with a State’s democratic processes, such as elections, responses to a national security or health emergency, such as the COVID-19 pandemic, and its choice of foreign policy.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,,
Costa Rica,CRI,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,No,"¶20. Such breaches may occur when cyber operations cause physical damage or loss of functionality of cyber infrastructure located in the victim State, regardless of ownership. Examples range from personal computers to programmable logic controllers or industrial computers that control energy, water, and sanitation facilities. ...For Costa Rica, a loss of functionality of these devices may occur in two ways. First, when the cyber operation attributable to another State entails the need to repair or replace physical components of the targeted cyber infrastructure or compromises physical equipment reliant on such infrastructure. Second, loss of functionality may occur if the operating system or database upon which the targeted cyber infrastructure relies stops functioning as intended, as may be the case, for instance, as a result of ransomware.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,‘Repair or replacement of physical components’ is one of the 2 ways in which loss of functionality can happen.,
Costa Rica,CRI,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,No,"¶20. Such breaches may occur when cyber operations cause physical damage or loss of functionality of cyber infrastructure located in the victim State, regardless of ownership. Examples range from personal computers to programmable logic controllers or industrial computers that control energy, water, and sanitation facilities. For Costa Rica, a loss of functionality of these devices may occur in two ways. First, when the cyber operation attributable to another State entails the need to repair or replace physical components of the targeted cyber infrastructure or compromises physical equipment reliant on such infrastructure.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,,
Costa Rica,CRI,7 Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶20. Such breaches may occur when cyber operations cause physical damage or loss of functionality of cyber infrastructure located in the victim State, regardless of ownership. Examples range from personal computers to programmable logic controllers or industrial computers that control energy, water, and sanitation facilities. For Costa Rica, a loss of functionality of these devices may occur in two ways. First, when the cyber operation attributable to another State entails the need to repair or replace physical components of the targeted cyber infrastructure or compromises physical equipment reliant on such infrastructure. Second, loss of functionality may occur if the operating system or database upon which the targeted cyber infrastructure relies stops functioning as intended, as may be the case, for instance, as a result of ransomware.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,,
Costa Rica,CRI,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"¶20. Second, loss of functionality may occur if the operating system or database  upon which the targeted cyber infrastructure relies stops functioning as intended, as may  be the case, for instance, as a result of ransomware","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,Costa Rica notes that loss of functionality “may” amount to a breach.  It does not specify that any such loss will constitute a breach.,
Costa Rica,CRI,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"¶20. Such breaches may occur when cyber operations cause physical damage or loss of functionality of cyber infrastructure located in the victim State, regardless of ownership. Examples range from personal computers to programmable logic controllers or industrial computers that control energy, water, and sanitation facilities.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,,
Costa Rica,CRI,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶21. Breaches of sovereignty may also occur when a State engages in cyber operations that constitute a usurpation of inherently governmental functions, irrespective of any physical or non-physical effects on hardware or software located in the territory of the victim State. Examples of cyber operations amounting to this type of violation are those interfering with a State’s democratic processes, such as elections, responses to a national security or health emergency, such as the COVID-19 pandemic, and its choice of foreign policy.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,,
Costa Rica,CRI,9. Must there be proximate causation between the state cyber operation and the effects or interference prohibited by sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,9(a) Must the state cyber operation generate prohibited effects directly?,Silent,,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,9(b) Do prohibited effects indirectly generated by a state cyber operation constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,10. Can state cyber operations that deny access to or degrade data in the control of another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶20. Such breaches may occur when cyber operations cause physical damage or loss of functionality of cyber infrastructure located in the victim State, regardless of ownership. Examples range from personal computers to programmable logic controllers or industrial computers that control energy, water, and sanitation facilities. For Costa Rica, a loss of functionality of these devices may occur in two ways. First, when the cyber operation attributable to another State entails the need to repair or replace physical components of the targeted cyber infrastructure or compromises physical equipment reliant on such infrastructure. Second, loss of functionality may occur if the operating system or database upon which the targeted cyber infrastructure relies stops functioning as intended, as may be the case, for instance, as a result of ransomware.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,,
Costa Rica,CRI,10(a) Can state sponsored ransomware operations violate the sovereignty of another State?,Yes,"¶20. Such breaches may occur when cyber operations cause physical damage or loss of functionality of cyber infrastructure located in the victim State, regardless of ownership. Examples range from personal computers to programmable logic controllers or industrial computers that control energy, water, and sanitation facilities. For Costa Rica, a loss of functionality of these devices may occur in two ways. First, when the cyber operation attributable to another State entails the need to repair or replace physical components of the targeted cyber infrastructure or compromises physical equipment reliant on such infrastructure. Second, loss of functionality may occur if the operating system or database upon which the targeted cyber infrastructure relies stops functioning as intended, as may be the case, for instance, as a result of ransomware.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,"May, not do.",
Costa Rica,CRI,10(b) Can state cyber operations against data residing in cloud servers outside of a State’s territorial jurisdiction constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,.,
Costa Rica,CRI,10(c) Does the exercise of one State’s law enforcement tools via cyber means in the networks or systems of another State’s territory violate the sovereignty of the latter State?,Silent,,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,11. Do State cyber operations that interfere at any level with critical infrastructure in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,No,"¶20. Such breaches may occur when cyber operations cause physical damage or loss of functionality of cyber infrastructure located in the victim State, regardless of ownership. Examples range from personal computers to programmable logic controllers or industrial computers that control energy, water, and sanitation facilities. For Costa Rica, a loss of functionality of these devices may occur in two ways. First, when the cyber operation attributable to another State entails the need to repair or replace physical components of the targeted cyber infrastructure or compromises physical equipment reliant on such infrastructure. Second, loss of functionality may occur if the operating system or database upon which the targeted cyber infrastructure relies stops functioning as intended, as may be the case, for instance, as a result of ransomware. ¶21. Breaches of sovereignty may also occur when a State engages in cyber operations that constitute a usurpation of inherently governmental functions, irrespective of any physical or non-physical effects on hardware or software located in the territory of the victim State.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,Costa Rica’s position does not set a different standard with respect to sovereignty breaches of critical infrastructure.  Interference “may” amount to a breach.,
Costa Rica,CRI,12. Can State cyber operations that cause significant national economic impacts for another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,13. Does cyber espionage by one State in or transiting networks or hardware resident in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"¶22. Furthermore, surveillance operations may be carried out in ways that lead to breaches of State sovereignty or other rules of international law. As such, Costa Rica believes that, in some circumstances, cyber espionage may amount to a breach of State sovereignty.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,,
Costa Rica,CRI,13(a) Can gaining unauthorized access to computer systems or data within a State’s sovereign control violate the rules of sovereignty?,Yes,"¶21. Breaches of sovereignty may also occur when a State engages in cyber operations that constitute a usurpation of inherently governmental functions, irrespective of any physical or non-physical effects on hardware or software located in the territory of the victim State.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,,
Costa Rica,CRI,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Silent,,,,,
Cuba,CUB,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"¶25. In this context, the implementation of the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations in cyberspace, particularly the principles of sovereign equality, territorial integrity, non-intervention in the internal affairs of States, refraining from the use or threat of use of force against the territorial integrity and political independence of States, international cooperation for mutual and equitable benefit and interest, and the peaceful settlement of disputes, acquires particular relevance.",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,/static/documents/22_position-paper-of-the-republic-of-cuba-on-the-application-of.pdf,,
Cuba,CUB,2.  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Silent,,,,,
Cuba,CUB,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a State to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Silent,"¶2. In principle, the use, or the permissibility of a misuse of ICTs that manifests itself in the territory of another State may, depending on its nature, the extent of the intrusion and its consequences, constitute a violation of sovereignty.",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,/static/documents/22_position-paper-of-the-republic-of-cuba-on-the-application-of.pdf,,
Cuba,CUB,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Silent,,,,,
Cuba,CUB,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"¶2. Ultimately, the violation of the obligation not to interfere in the internal affairs of other States is established irrespective of whether Information and Communication Technologies (ICTs) or otherwise were use.  In principle, the use, or the permissibility of a misuse of ICTs that manifests itself in the territory of another State may, depending on its nature, the extent of the intrusion and its consequences, constitute a violation of sovereignty.",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,/static/documents/22_position-paper-of-the-republic-of-cuba-on-the-application-of.pdf,,
Cuba,CUB,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Ambiguous,"¶ 2. In principle, the use, or the permissibility of a misuse of ICTs that manifests itself in the territory of another State may, depending on its nature, the extent of the intrusion and its consequences, constitute a violation of sovereignty. ¶ 15. Malicious use of ICT may constitute an internationally wrongful act, without this implying that it is equated to the use of force. States should have clear primary international obligations on the matter that also have an impact on other subjects such as large technology companies and eventual independent actors, potential triggers of an international conflict through the malicious use of ICT.",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,/static/documents/22_position-paper-of-the-republic-of-cuba-on-the-application-of.pdf,,
Cuba,CUB,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,No,,,,,
Cuba,CUB,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Silent,,,,,
Cuba,CUB,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Ambiguous,"¶2. Ultimately, the violation of the obligation not to interfere in the internal affairs of other States is established irrespective of whether Information and Communication Technologies (ICTs) or otherwise were use.  In principle, the use, or the permissibility of a misuse of ICTs that manifests itself in the territory of another State may, depending on its nature, the extent of the intrusion and its consequences, constitute a violation of sovereignty.",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,/static/documents/22_position-paper-of-the-republic-of-cuba-on-the-application-of.pdf,,
