Czech Republic,CZE,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"¶1. The principles of sovereignty and sovereign equality of States are fundamental concepts of international law, cornerstones of the UN Charter and apply in cyberspace, just as they do in other domains. First, the Czech Republic recalls that the principles of sovereignty and sovereign equality of States are cornerstones of the UN Charter",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace Statement of the Special Envoy for Cyberspace and Director of Cybersecurity Department of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/68JC-6HQ6,,
Czech Republic,CZE,2.  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"¶2. The two facets of sovereignty are the internal facet, meaning that every State exercises its sovereignty over its territory, including the ICTs located therein, subject to its obligations under international law, including international human rights law, and the external facet, meaning that every State may freely and independently choose and develop its political, social, economic and cultural system and determine its foreign policy. The Czech Republic firmly believes that under this principle States may freely exercise without interference in any form by another State both aspects of sovereignty in cyberspace, be it an internal one, with the exclusive jurisdiction over the ICTs located on its territory.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace Statement of the Special Envoy for Cyberspace and Director of Cybersecurity Department of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/68JC-6HQ6,,
Czech Republic,CZE,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a State to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"¶2. The two facets of sovereignty are the internal facet, meaning that every State exercises its sovereignty over its territory, including the ICTs located therein” The Czech Republic firmly believes that under this principle States may freely exercise without interference in any form by another State both aspects of sovereignty in cyberspace, be it an internal one, with the exclusive jurisdiction over the ICTs located on its territory.”]",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace Statement of the Special Envoy for Cyberspace and Director of Cybersecurity Department of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/68JC-6HQ6,The answer can be inferred from the statement.,
Czech Republic,CZE,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"¶2. The two facets of sovereignty are the internal facet, meaning that every State exercises its sovereignty over its territory, including the ICTs located therein, subject to its obligations under international law, including international human rights law, and the external facet, meaning that every State may freely and independently choose and develop its political, social, economic and cultural system and determine its foreign policy...",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,,
Czech Republic,CZE,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"¶3. The sovereignty of every State must be respected. The principle of sovereignty forms a basis of various rules, such as the prohibition of intervention and the prohibition of the use of force. At the same time, sovereignty is a rule in its own right which is capable of being violated. Thus, cyber activities that do not amount to a prohibited intervention or a prohibited use of force may nevertheless amount to a violation of a State’s sovereignty under international law. In such case, violation of one State’s sovereignty by another State, including by a cyber means, would be considered an internationally wrongful act.”",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,,
Czech Republic,CZE,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"¶3.... sovereignty is a rule in its own right which is capable of being violated. Thus, cyber activities that do not amount to a prohibited intervention or a prohibited use of force may nevertheless amount to a violation of a State’s sovereignty under international law. In such case, violation of one State’s sovereignty by another State, including by a cyber means, would be considered an internationally wrongful act.”",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,,
Czech Republic,CZE,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"¶5. The Czech Republic is of the view that cyber activities that rise above the level of negligible or de minimis effects and cause significant harmful effects on the territory of another State without the consent of that State could amount to a violation of the rule of sovereignty in relation to the State concerned. While there is no universal consensus on the threshold, the Czech Republic is of the opinion that the analysis should take into account the degree of infringement upon the target State’s territorial integrity (i.e. the scope, scale, impact or severity of the effects of the operation and the causal link between the operation and its effects) or whether the operation interfered with or usurped inherently governmental functions. In general, the impact or severity of cyber effects will be evaluated in the same manner and according to the same criteria as for physical activities.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,,
Czech Republic,CZE,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Yes,¶5. The Czech Republic is of the view that cyber activities that rise above the level of negligible or de minimis effects and cause significant harmful effects on the territory of another State without the consent of that State could amount to a violation of the rule of sovereignty in relation to the State concerned.,Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,,
Czech Republic,CZE,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Ambiguous,"The Czech Republic considers the following cyber operations in a State’s territory as violation of its sovereignty, if attributable to another State:  ... D. cyber operation against a State or entities or persons located therein, including international organizations [sic], conducted by a physically present organ of another State.","Statement of the Special Envoy for Cyberspace and Director of Cybersecurity Department of the Czech Republic,",https://perma.cc/68JC-6HQ6,,
Czech Republic,CZE,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶5. In general, the impact or severity of cyber effects will be evaluated in the same manner and according to the same criteria as for physical activities... ¶6. The Czech Republic is of the opinion that the following cyber operations in one State’s territory constitute violations of sovereignty, if attributed to another State, inter alia:  a) a cyber operation causing death or injury to persons or significant physical damage; The Czech Republic considers the following cyber operations in a State’s territory as violation of its sovereignty, if attributable to another State: A. a cyber operation causing death or injury to persons or significant physical damage;",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace Statement of the Special Envoy for Cyberspace and Director of Cybersecurity Department of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/68JC-6HQ6,Threshold analysis would apply,
Czech Republic,CZE,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,No,¶5. The Czech Republic is of the view that cyber activities that rise above the level of negligible or de minimis effects and cause significant harmful effects on the territory of another State without the consent of that State could amount to a violation of the rule of sovereignty in relation to the State concerned.,Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,,
Czech Republic,CZE,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"¶6. The Czech Republic is of the opinion that the following cyber operations in one State’s territory constitute violations of sovereignty, if attributed to another State, inter alia: ... c) a cyber operation interfering with any data or services which are essential for the exercise of inherently governmental functions, and thereby significantly disrupting the exercise of those functions; for example, distributing ransomware which encrypts the computers used by a government and thus disables the payment of retirement pensions or other social benefits.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,,
Czech Republic,CZE,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,No,"¶6. The Czech Republic is of the opinion that the following cyber operations in one State’s territory constitute violations of sovereignty, if attributed to another State, inter alia:” ... “ b) a cyber operation causing damage to or disruption of cyber or other infrastructure with a significant impact on national security, economy, public health, public safety or environment; for instance, if such an operation causes a severe power outage affecting thousands of households; c) a cyber operation interfering with any data or services which are essential for the exercise of inherently governmental functions, and thereby significantly disrupting the exercise of those functions; for example, distributing ransomware which encrypts the computers used by a government and thus disables the payment of retirement pensions or other social benefits.”",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,,
Czech Republic,CZE,7 Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶6. The Czech Republic is of the opinion that the following cyber operations in one State’s territory constitute violations of sovereignty, if attributed to another State, inter alia:” ... “ b) a cyber operation causing damage to or disruption of cyber or other infrastructure with a significant impact on national security, economy, public health, public safety or environment; for instance, if such an operation causes a severe power outage affecting thousands of households; c) a cyber operation interfering with any data or services which are essential for the exercise of inherently governmental functions, and thereby significantly disrupting the exercise of those functions; for example, distributing ransomware which encrypts the computers used by a government and thus disables the payment of retirement pensions or other social benefits.”",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,,
Czech Republic,CZE,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,No,"¶6. The Czech Republic is of the opinion that the following cyber operations in one State’s territory constitute violations of sovereignty, if attributed to another State, inter alia:”... “ b) a cyber operation causing damage to or disruption of cyber or other infrastructure with a significant impact on national security, economy, public health, public safety or environment; for instance, if such an operation causes a severe power outage affecting thousands of households; c) a cyber operation interfering with any data or services which are essential for the exercise of inherently governmental functions, and thereby significantly disrupting the exercise of those functions; for example, distributing ransomware which encrypts the computers used by a government and thus disables the payment of retirement pensions or other social benefits.”",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,,
Czech Republic,CZE,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"¶6. The Czech Republic is of the opinion that the following cyber operations in one State’s territory constitute violations of sovereignty, if attributed to another State, inter alia:”... “ b) a cyber operation causing damage to or disruption of cyber or other infrastructure with a significant impact on national security, economy, public health, public safety or environment; for instance, if such an operation causes a severe power outage affecting thousands of households; c) a cyber operation interfering with any data or services which are essential for the exercise of inherently governmental functions, and thereby significantly disrupting the exercise of those functions; for example, distributing ransomware which encrypts the computers used by a government and thus disables the payment of retirement pensions or other social benefits.”",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,"The functionality loss appears to have to be connected to other significant impacts.  A significant disruption of the exercise of inherently governmental functions through cyber interference with data or services, as well as the disruption of cyber infrastructure, are both presented as examples of sovereignty violations. The position paper, therefore, suggests that a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within a state’s territory may violate the rule if inherently governmental functions are disrupted or a significant impact on a consideration of sovereignty results.",
Czech Republic,CZE,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶6. The Czech Republic is of the opinion that the following cyber operations in one State’s territory constitute violations of sovereignty, if attributed to another State, inter alia:”... c) a cyber operation interfering with any data or services which are essential for the exercise of inherently governmental functions, and thereby significantly disrupting the exercise of those functions; for example, distributing ransomware which encrypts the computers used by a government and thus disables the payment of retirement pensions or other social benefits.”",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,,
Czech Republic,CZE,9. Must there be proximate causation between the state cyber operation and the effects or interference prohibited by sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"¶5. The Czech Republic is of the view that cyber activities that rise above the level of negligible or de minimis effects and cause significant harmful effects on the territory of another State without the consent of that State could amount to a violation of the rule of sovereignty in relation to the State concerned. While there is no universal consensus on the threshold, the Czech Republic is of the opinion that the analysis should take into account the degree of infringement upon the target State’s territorial integrity (i.e. the scope, scale, impact or severity of the effects of the operation and the causal link between the operation and its effects) or whether the operation interfered with or usurped inherently governmental functions.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,"Although the position paper vaguely recommends that the violation analysis should consider the causal link, the legal language of proximate causation is not specifically discussed. The paper outlines specific criteria for determining whether a cyber operation violates sovereignty, including the scale and severity of the impact and the context in which it occurs.",
