Czech Republic,CZE,10. Can state cyber operations that deny access to or degrade data in the control of another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶6. The Czech Republic is of the opinion that the following cyber operations in one State’s territory constitute violations of sovereignty, if attributed to another State, inter alia:”...    c) a cyber operation interfering with any data or services which are essential for the exercise of inherently governmental functions, and thereby significantly disrupting the exercise of those functions; for example, distributing ransomware which encrypts the computers used by a government and thus disables the payment of retirement pensions or other social benefits.”    Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, ¶6 (2024).",,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,10(a) Can state sponsored ransomware operations violate the sovereignty of another State?,Yes,"¶6. The Czech Republic is of the opinion that the following cyber operations in one State’s territory constitute violations of sovereignty, if attributed to another State, inter alia: ... c) a cyber operation interfering with any data or services which are essential for the exercise of inherently governmental functions, and thereby significantly disrupting the exercise of those functions; for example, distributing ransomware which encrypts the computers used by a government and thus disables the payment of retirement pensions or other social benefits.”",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,,
Czech Republic,CZE,10(b) Can state cyber operations against data residing in cloud servers outside of a State’s territorial jurisdiction constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,10(c) Does the exercise of one State’s law enforcement tools via cyber means in the networks or systems of another State’s territory violate the sovereignty of the latter State?,Ambiguous,"¶4.... the Czech Republic does not consider every cyber operation attributable to a State and having an effect in the territory or infrastructure of another State to be a violation of the latter’s sovereignty. Cyberspace is a global and open domain and States often exercise their jurisdiction in another State’s territory without legal repercussions. For instance, an organ of a State viewing a publicly available website hosted in another State for the purposes of obtaining information during a criminal investigation does not violate that State’s sovereignty, despite exercising jurisdiction in its territory without its consent.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,"The position paper leaves room to interpret that certain law enforcement tools, including online criminal investigations, may not violate sovereignty, but it would depend on the degree of infringement by the cyber means.",
Czech Republic,CZE,11. Do State cyber operations that interfere at any level with critical infrastructure in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,No,"¶6. The Czech Republic is of the opinion that the following cyber operations in one State’s territory constitute violations of sovereignty, if attributed to another State, inter alia:... b) a cyber operation causing damage to or disruption of cyber or other infrastructure with a significant impact on national security, economy, public health, public safety or environment; for instance, if such an operation causes a severe power outage affecting thousands of households;",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,"Interference with critical infrastructure may constitute a violation, subject to the threshold requirement.",
Czech Republic,CZE,12. Can State cyber operations that cause significant national economic impacts for another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶6. The Czech Republic is of the opinion that the following cyber operations in one State’s territory constitute violations of sovereignty, if attributed to another State, inter alia: ... b) a cyber operation causing damage to or disruption of cyber or other infrastructure with a significant impact on national security, economy, public health, public safety or environment; for instance, if such an operation causes a severe power outage affecting thousands of households;",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,,
Czech Republic,CZE,13. Does cyber espionage by one State in or transiting networks or hardware resident in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,13(a) Can gaining unauthorized access to computer systems or data within a State’s sovereign control violate the rules of sovereignty?,Silent,,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Yes,"¶5. The Czech Republic will assess whether a violation of sovereignty has occurred on a case-by-case basis, since further State practice and opinio juris is needed to clarify the scope of customary law in this area over time.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,,
Denmark,DNK,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,Denmark is of the view that sovereignty is not only a principle but a primary rule of international law a breach of which amounts to an internationally wrongful act and if attributable to a State it may give rise to State responsibility.,Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"It follows that all States may exercise sovereignty over any cyber infrastructure located on their territory and all activities associated with that infrastructure – irrespective of whether such infrastructure or activity is of a public or private character. In the exercise of governmental authority, the State may promulgate and enforce domestic laws or protect cyber infrastructure and cyber activity located or taking place in its territory unless prohibited from doing so by its international legal obligations such as the limitations set out in international human rights conventions and international law on State and diplomatic immunity.",Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a State to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"It follows that all States may exercise sovereignty over any cyber infrastructure located on their territory and all activities associated with that infrastructure – irrespective of whether such infrastructure or activity is of a public or private character. In the exercise of governmental authority, the State may promulgate and enforce domestic laws or protect cyber infrastructure and cyber activity located or taking place in its territory unless prohibited from doing so by its international legal obligations such as the limitations set out in international human rights conventions and international law on State and diplomatic immunity.",Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"In the exercise of governmental authority, the State may promulgate and enforce domestic laws or protect cyber infrastructure and cyber activity located or taking place in its territory unless prohibited from doing so by its international legal obligations such as the limitations set out in international human rights conventions and international law on State and diplomatic immunity.",Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,Denmark is of the view that sovereignty is not only a principle but a primary rule of international law a breach of which amounts to an internationally wrongful act and if attributable to a State it may give rise to State responsibility.,Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"“Unlike the prohibition on intervention, a breach of sovereignty is not contingent on a coercion element... Whether or not a given act in cyberspace is done in violation of another State’s sovereignty requires a case-by-case assessment of all relevant factors, in particular the nature of and the effects caused by the cyber operation... With respect to infringements on a State’s territory Denmark generally shares the view that cyber operations which result in physical damage or injury constitute a violation of a State’s sovereignty and may also violate the principle of non-intervention, or the prohibition of the use of force... In addition to physical damage or injury loss of functionality may also,  depending on its nature, scale, and effects, constitute such a violation. Cyber operations that alter or delete data without necessarily resulting in physical damage or loss of functionality may also, based on a case-by-case assessment..",Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"Whether or not a given act in cyberspace is done in violation of another State’s sovereignty requires a case-by-case assessment of all relevant factors, in particular the nature of and the effects caused by the cyber operation",Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur,Yes,Cyber activities causing negligible physical effects or loss of functionality  would generally not be considered a violation of sovereignty.,Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Denmark,DNK,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"With respect to infringements on a State’s territory Denmark generally shares the view that cyber operations which result in physical damage or injury constitute a violation of a State’s sovereignty and may also violate the principle of non-intervention, or the prohibition of the use of force.",Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,No,Cyber activities causing negligible physical effects or loss of functionality would generally not be considered a violation of sovereignty.,Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"Furthermore, interference with or usurpation of a State’s inherently governmental functions may constitute a violation of a State’s sovereignty or prohibited intervention.",Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,,
Denmark,DNK,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,No,"Furthermore, interference with or usurpation of a State’s inherently governmental functions may constitute a violation of a State’s sovereignty or prohibited intervention.",Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,7 Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"With respect to infringements on a State’s territory Denmark generally  shares the view that cyber operations which result in physical damage or  injury constitute a violation of a State’s sovereignty and may also violate the principle of non-intervention, or the prohibition of the use of force, cf. section 3 and 4. In addition to physical damage or injury loss of functionality may also, depending on its nature, scale, and effects, constitute such a violation.",Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,No,Cyber activities causing negligible physical effects or loss of functionality would generally not be considered a violation of sovereignty.,Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"In addition to physical damage or injury loss of functionality may also, depending on its nature, scale, and effects, constitute such a violation.",Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Denmark,DNK,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"Furthermore, interference with or usurpation of a State’s inherently governmental functions may constitute a violation of a State’s sovereignty or prohibited intervention. This assessment is not contingent on whether physical damage, injury, or loss of functionality have occurred, but rather if a cyber operation has interfered with data or services necessary for the exercise of inherently governmental functions. This applies irrespective of whether such inherently governmental functions are performed by the State itself (either by central, regional or local government) or have been delegated to non-governmental entities.",Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,9. Must there be proximate causation between the state cyber operation and the effects or interference prohibited by sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Denmark,DNK,9(a) Must the state cyber operation generate prohibited effects directly?,Silent,,,,,
Denmark,DNK,9(b) Do prohibited effects indirectly generated by a state cyber operation constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Denmark,DNK,10. Can state cyber operations that deny access to or degrade data in the control of another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"In addition to physical damage or injury loss of functionality may also,  depending on its nature, scale, and effects, constitute such a violation. Cyber operations that alter or delete data without necessarily resulting in physical damage or loss of functionality may also, based on a case-by-case assessment of the nature, scale, and effects of the operation in question, constitute a violation.",Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,10(a) Do state sponsored ransomware operations violate the sovereignty of another State?,Silent,,,,,
Denmark,DNK,10(b) Can state cyber operations against data residing in cloud servers outside of a State’s territorial jurisdiction constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Denmark,DNK,10(c) Does the exercise of one State’s law enforcement tools via cyber means in the networks or systems of another State’s territory violate the sovereignty of the latter State?,Silent,,,,,
Denmark,DNK,11. Do State cyber operations that interfere at any level with critical infrastructure in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,No,Cyber activities causing negligible physical effects or loss of functionality would generally not be considered a violation of sovereignty.,Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
Denmark,DNK,12. Can State cyber operations that cause significant national economic impacts for another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Denmark,DNK,13. Does cyber espionage by one State in or transiting networks or hardware resident in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Denmark,DNK,13(a) Can gaining unauthorized access to computer systems or data within a State’s sovereign control violate the rules of sovereignty?,Silent,,,,,
Denmark,DNK,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Yes,"Whether or not a given act in cyberspace is done in violation of another State’s sovereignty requires a case-by-case assessment of all relevant factors, in particular the nature of and the effects caused by the cyber operation.",Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,,
