Estonia,EST,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,Sovereignty as a fundamental principle of international law applies in cyberspace.,"Estonia, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Estonia,EST,2.  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,¶1. States have territorial sovereignty over the ICT infrastructure and persons engaged in cyber activities on their territory.,"Estonia, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Estonia,EST,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,¶1. States have territorial sovereignty over the ICT infrastructure and persons engaged in cyber activities on their territory.,"Estonia, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,Answer can be inferred from the statement.,
Estonia,EST,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"¶1. States have territorial sovereignty over the ICT infrastructure and persons engaged in cyber activities on their territory. However, states’ right to exercise sovereignty on their territory is not unlimited; states must respect international law, including human rights obligations. States also bear the responsibility to comply with legal obligations flowing from sovereignty – for example, the responsibility not to breach the sovereignty of other states and to take reasonable efforts to ensure that their territory is not used to adversely affect the rights of other states”","Estonia, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Estonia,EST,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"¶2. The violation of sovereignty through cyber means can breach international law, and therefore may give the victim state the right to take measures, including countermeasures.","Estonia, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Estonia,EST,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"¶2. The violation of sovereignty through cyber means can breach international law, and therefore may give the victim state the right to take measures, including countermeasures.","Estonia, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Estonia,EST,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,No,,,,,
Estonia,EST,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Silent,,,,,
Estonia,EST,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Estonia,EST,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"¶2. The violation of sovereignty through cyber means can breach international law, and therefore may give the victim state the right to take measures, including countermeasures.  Malicious cyber operations can be complex, cross several jurisdictions and may not always produce physical effects on targeted infrastructure.","Estonia, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Estonia,EST,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Silent,,,,,
Estonia,EST,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",Ambiguous,"¶2. The violation of sovereignty through cyber means can breach international law, and therefore may give the victim state the right to take measures, including countermeasures.  Malicious cyber operations can be complex, cross several jurisdictions and may not always produce physical effects on targeted infrastructure.","Estonia, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
