European Union,EUN,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,§1. State sovereignty is a core principle of international law.,Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,,
European Union,EUN,2.  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"§1. States exercise territorial jurisdiction over Information and Communications Technology (ICT) infrastructure located in their territory, and persons engaged in cyber activities, within their territory.",Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,,
European Union,EUN,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"§1. States are entitled to exercise territorial jurisdiction over persons, property and activities within their territories… States exercise territorial jurisdiction over… ICT infrastructure… and persons engaged in cyber activities, within their territory.",Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,Answer can be inferred from the statement.,
European Union,EUN,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"§1. The European Union and its Member States reaffirm that international law, in particular the UN Charter, international human rights law and international humanitarian law, fully applies to cyberspace. §6. States must comply with their obligations under international human rights law online just as offline. States are under a negative obligation to refrain from acting in violation of human rights, as well as a positive obligation to actively protect the rights of persons within their jurisdiction against such violations.”",Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,,
European Union,EUN,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"§1. The violation of the obligation to respect State sovereignty amounts to an internationally wrongful act, even if such a violation does not constitute a prohibited intervention or a prohibited use of force.”",Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,,
European Union,EUN,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"§1. The violation of the obligation to respect State sovereignty amounts to an internationally wrongful act, even if such a violation does not constitute a prohibited intervention or a prohibited use of force.”",Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,,
European Union,EUN,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"§1. A violation of the obligation to respect sovereignty may occur when a cyber operation, attributable to a State, infringes upon another State’s territorial integrity or leads to interference with or usurpation of inherently governmental functions of another State. §3.…coercive interference with ICT systems, cloud-services and networks on another State’s territory or within its jurisdiction without its consent, within its domaine réservé, can constitute a prohibited intervention under international law if it is attributable to a State.",Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,,
European Union,EUN,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"§1. A violation of the obligation to respect sovereignty may occur when a cyber operation, attributable to a State, infringes upon another State’s territorial integrity or leads to interference with or usurpation of inherently governmental functions of another State.",Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,Answer inferred from quoted passage.,
European Union,EUN,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"§1. A violation of the obligation to respect sovereignty may occur when a cyber operation, attributable to a State, infringes upon another State’s territorial integrity or leads to interference with or usurpation of inherently governmental functions of another State.",Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,,
European Union,EUN,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,Silent,,,,,
