European Union,EUN,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"§1. A violation of the obligation to respect sovereignty may occur when a cyber operation, attributable to a State, infringes upon another State’s territorial integrity or leads to interference with or usurpation of inherently governmental functions of another State.",Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,,
European Union,EUN,9. Must there be proximate causation between the state cyber operation and the effects or interference prohibited by sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,9(a) Must the state cyber operation generate prohibited effects directly?,Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,9(b) Do prohibited effects indirectly generated by a state cyber operation constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,10. Can state cyber operations that deny access to or degrade data in the control of another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,10(a) Can state sponsored ransomware operations violate the sovereignty of another State?,Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,10(b) Can state cyber operations against data residing in cloud servers outside of a State’s territorial jurisdiction constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,10(c) Does the exercise of one State’s law enforcement tools via cyber means in the networks or systems of another State’s territory violate the sovereignty of the latter State?,Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,11. Do State cyber operations that interfere at any level with critical infrastructure in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,12. Can State cyber operations that cause significant national economic impacts for another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,13. Does cyber espionage by one State in or transiting networks or hardware resident in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,13(a) Can gaining unauthorized access to computer systems or data within a State’s sovereign control violate the rules of sovereignty?,Silent,,,,,
European Union,EUN,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Silent,,,,,
Finland,FIN,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"In this sense, sovereignty is a foundational principle of the international legal order.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
Finland,FIN,2.  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"Sovereignty confers each State the exclusive right to exercise the functions of a State within a certain territory, and protects its territorial integrity and political independence from interference by other States.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,"It is plainly stated that the entire field of cyberspace by its nature cannot be governed by any one state, but cyber infrastructure residing in a state can be regulated by the state.",
Finland,FIN,"2(a). Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"While cyberspace as a whole cannot be subject to appropriation by any State, each State has jurisdiction over the cyber infrastructure and the persons engaged in cyber activities within its territory.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,Answer can be inferred from the statement.,
Finland,FIN,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"It should nevertheless be underlined that individuals enjoy the same international human rights with respect to cyber-related activities as otherwise and, accordingly, that States are bound by all their human rights obligations both online and offline.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
Finland,FIN,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"Finland sees sovereignty as a primary rule of international law, a breach of which amounts to an internationally wrongful act and triggers State responsibility.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
Finland,FIN,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"Below the threshold of prohibited intervention, all States have an obligation to refrain from acts that violate the territorial integrity or political independence of other States.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
Finland,FIN,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"Similarly, a non-consensual intrusion in the computer networks and systems that rely on the cyber infrastructure in another State’s territory may amount to a violation of that State’s sovereignty. The prohibition of cyber operations violating the territorial sovereignty of another State protects, first of all, the cyber infrastructure located in the territory of that State, or otherwise under its jurisdiction, as well as computer networks and systems supported by such infrastructure, from material harm. The situation is the same irrespective of whether such infrastructure belongs to or is operated by governmental institutions, private entities or private individuals. In addition to material harm that may be caused by such an operation, other relevant considerations include whether an intrusion in the cyber infrastructure triggers a loss of  functionality of the equipment relying on it, or modifies or deletes information belonging to the  target State, or to private actors in its territory.  More generally, an unauthorized intrusion by cyber means may be seen as a violation of the target State’s territorial sovereignty if it interferes with data or services that are necessary for the exercise of inherently governmental functions. This rule put forward in the Tallinn Manual 2.0 is consistent with the understanding of violations of sovereignty as unauthorized exercise of authority in another State’s territory.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
Finland,FIN,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Ambiguous,"Similarly, a non-consensual intrusion in the computer networks and systems that rely on the  cyber infrastructure in another State’s territory may amount to a violation of that State’s  sovereignty. The prohibition of cyber operations violating the territorial sovereignty of another State protects, first of all, the cyber infrastructure located in the territory of that State, or  otherwise under its jurisdiction, as well as computer networks and systems supported by such  infrastructure, from material harm. The situation is the same irrespective of whether such infrastructure belongs to or is operated by governmental institutions, private entities or private  individuals. In addition to material harm that may be caused by such an operation, other relevant  considerations include whether an intrusion in the cyber infrastructure triggers a loss of  functionality of the equipment relying on it, or modifies or deletes information belonging to the target State, or to private actors in its territory. governmental functions.”",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
Finland,FIN,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Finland,FIN,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"The prohibition of cyber operations violating the territorial sovereignty of another State protects, first of all, the cyber infrastructure located in the territory of that State, or otherwise under its jurisdiction, as well as computer networks and systems supported by such infrastructure, from material harm.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
Finland,FIN,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Ambiguous,"Similarly, a non-consensual intrusion in the computer networks and systems that rely on the cyber infrastructure in another State’s territory may amount to a violation of that State’s  sovereignty.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,"Read holistically, “material harm” “may” constitute a violation.",
Finland,FIN,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"In addition to material harm that may be caused by such an operation, other relevant considerations include whether an intrusion in the cyber infrastructure triggers a loss of functionality of the equipment relying on it, or modifies or deletes information belonging to the target State, or to private actors in its territory.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
Finland,FIN,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,,
Finland,FIN,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,Ambiguous,"The prohibition of cyber operations violating the territorial sovereignty of another State protects, first of all, the cyber infrastructure located in the territory of that State, or otherwise under its jurisdiction, as well as computer networks and systems supported by such infrastructure, from material harm.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
Finland,FIN,7 Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"...other relevant considerations include whether an intrusion in the cyber infrastructure triggers a loss of functionality of the equipment relying on it, or modifies or deletes information belonging to the target State, or to private actors in its territory.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
Finland,FIN,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,No,"Similarly, a non-consensual intrusion in the computer networks and systems that rely on the cyber infrastructure in another State’s territory may amount to a violation of that State’s  sovereignty  ...other relevant considerations include whether an intrusion in the cyber infrastructure triggers a loss of functionality of the equipment relying on it or modifies or deletes information belonging to the target State, or to private actors in its territory.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
Finland,FIN,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"Similarly, a non-consensual intrusion in the computer networks and systems that rely on the cyber infrastructure in another State’s territory may amount to a violation of that State’s sovereignty. ...other relevant considerations include whether an intrusion in the cyber infrastructure triggers a loss of functionality of the equipment relying on it, or modifies or deletes information belonging to the target State, or to private actors in its territory.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
Finland,FIN,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Finland,FIN,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"More generally, an unauthorized intrusion by cyber means may be seen as a violation of the target State’s territorial sovereignty if it interferes with data or services that are necessary for the exercise of inherently governmental functions.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
