Finland,FIN,10(c) Does the exercise of one State’s law enforcement tools via cyber means in the networks or systems of another State’s territory violate the sovereignty of the latter State?,Silent,,,,,
Finland,FIN,11. Do State cyber operations that interfere at any level with critical infrastructure in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Finland,FIN,12. Can State cyber operations that cause significant national economic impacts for another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,Whether an unauthorized cyber intrusion violates the target State’s sovereignty  depends on its nature and consequences and is subject to a case-by-case assessment.,International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
Finland,FIN,13. Does cyber espionage by one State in or transiting networks or hardware resident in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Finland,FIN,13(a) Can gaining unauthorized access to computer systems or data within a State’s sovereign control violate the rules of sovereignty?,Yes,"Similarly, a non-consensual intrusion in the computer networks and systems that rely on the cyber infrastructure in another State’s territory may amount to a violation of that State’s sovereignty.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
Finland,FIN,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Yes,Whether an unauthorized cyber intrusion violates the target State’s sovereignty depends on its nature and consequences and is subject to a case-by-case assessment.,International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,,
France,FRA,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,The principle of sovereignty applies to cyberspace. France exercises its sovereignty over the information systems located on its territory.,"International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",/static/documents/02_international-law-applied-to-operations-in-cyberspace-paper.pdf,Inferrered from quoted language and statement.,
France,FRA,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"§ 1.1.1. France exercises its sovereignty over the information systems located on its territory. In compliance with the due diligence requirement, it ensures that its territory is not used for internationally wrongful acts using ICTs.","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",/static/documents/02_international-law-applied-to-operations-in-cyberspace-paper.pdf,,
France,FRA,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Ambiguous,"§ 1.1. States targeted by such cyberattacks are entitled to respond to them within the framework of the options offered by international law. In response to a cyberattack, France may consider diplomatic responses to certain incidents, counter-measures, or even coercive action by the armed forces if an attack constitutes armed aggression.","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",/static/documents/02_international-law-applied-to-operations-in-cyberspace-paper.pdf,"Within the statement, France held they retained the authority and ability to safeguard their cyberinfrastructure, including the implementation of countermeasures. Furthermore, they interpeted international law only permitted the use of countermeasures in situations where nations are themselves victims, and barred such action in the interest of another nation.",
France,FRA,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Silent,,,,,
France,FRA,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"§1.1. Many States are acquiring the capacity to prepare and conduct operations in cyberspace. When carried out to the detriment of the rights of other States, such operations may breach international law. Depending on the extent of their intrusion or their effects, they may violate the principles of sovereignty, non-intervention or even the prohibition of the threat or use of force.","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",/static/documents/02_international-law-applied-to-operations-in-cyberspace-paper.pdf,,
France,FRA,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"§1.1. Many States are acquiring the capacity to prepare and conduct operations in cyberspace. When carried out to the detriment of the rights of other States, such operations may breach international law. Depending on the extent of their intrusion or their effects, they may violate the principles of sovereignty, non-intervention or even the prohibition of the threat or use of force.","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",/static/documents/02_international-law-applied-to-operations-in-cyberspace-paper.pdf,"By making mention how the extent of a hostile state’s intrusion can give rise to further breaches of international law, the converse is implied, that detrimental cyberoperations can take place that are internationally wrongful acts that aren’t prohibited interventions",
France,FRA,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"§1.1.1. Any cyberattack against French digital systems or any effects produced on French territory by digital means by a State organ, a person or an entity exercising elements of governmental authority or by a person or persons acting on the instructions of or under the direction or control of a State constitutes a breach of sovereignty.","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",/static/documents/02_international-law-applied-to-operations-in-cyberspace-paper.pdf,,
France,FRA,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Silent,,,,,
France,FRA,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
France,FRA,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"§1.1.1. Any cyberattack against French digital systems or any effects produced on French territory by digitalmeans by a State organ, a person or an entity exercising elements of governmental authority or by a person or persons acting on the instructions of or under the direction or control of a State constitutes a breach of sovereignty.","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",/static/documents/02_international-law-applied-to-operations-in-cyberspace-paper.pdf,,
France,FRA,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Ambiguous,"§1.1.1. Any cyberattack against French digital systems or any effects produced on French territory by digital means by a State organ, a person or an entity exercising elements of governmental authority or by a person or persons acting on the instructions of or under the direction or control of a State constitutes a breach of sovereignty.","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",/static/documents/02_international-law-applied-to-operations-in-cyberspace-paper.pdf,"Internally inconsistent provisions within the statement, as well as the use of the term cyberattacks.",
France,FRA,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"§1.1.1. Any cyberattack against French digital systems or any effects produced on French territory by digitalmeans by a State organ, a person or an entity exercising elements of governmental authority or by a person or persons acting on the instructions of or under the direction or control of a State constitutes a breach of sovereignty.","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",/static/documents/02_international-law-applied-to-operations-in-cyberspace-paper.pdf,,
France,FRA,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,,
France,FRA,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,No,"§1.1.1. Any cyberattack against French digital systems or any effects produced on French territory by digitalmeans by a State organ, a person or an entity exercising elements of governmental authority or by a person or persons acting on the instructions of or under the direction or control of a State constitutes a breach of sovereignty.","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",/static/documents/02_international-law-applied-to-operations-in-cyberspace-paper.pdf,,
France,FRA,6(d) Does a violation of the rule of sovereignty require that a cyber operation generate death or injury to individuals or damage or destruction to objects within another State’s territory?,No,"§1.1.1. Any cyberattack against French digital systems or any effects produced on French territory by digitalmeans by a State organ, a person or an entity exercising elements of governmental authority or by a person or persons acting on the instructions of or under the direction or control of a State constitutes a breach of sovereignty.","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",/static/documents/02_international-law-applied-to-operations-in-cyberspace-paper.pdf,,
France,FRA,7 Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"§1.1.1. Any cyberattack against French digital systems or any effects produced on French territory by digitalmeans by a State organ, a person or an entity exercising elements of governmental authority or by a person or persons acting on the instructions of or under the direction or control of a State constitutes a breach of sovereignty. §2.2.1. Contrary to the definition given by the Tallinn Manual Group of Experts50, France does not characterise a cyberattack solely on the basis of material criteria. It considers that a cyberoperation is an attack where the targeted equipment or systems no longer provide the service for which they were implemented, whether temporarily or permanently, reversibly or not. If the effects are temporary and/or reversible, the attack is characterised where action by the adversary is necessary to restore the infrastructure or system (repair of equipment, replacement of a part, reinstallation of a network, etc.).","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",/static/documents/02_international-law-applied-to-operations-in-cyberspace-paper.pdf,,
France,FRA,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"§1.1.1. Any cyberattack against French digital systems or any effects produced on French territory by digitalmeans by a State organ, a person or an entity exercising elements of governmental authority or by a person or persons acting on the instructions of or under the direction or control of a State constitutes a breach of sovereignty. §2.2.1. Contrary to the definition given by the Tallinn Manual Group of Experts50, France does not characterise a cyberattack solely on the basis of material criteria. It considers that a cyberoperation is an attack where the targeted equipment or systems no longer provide the service for which they were implemented, whether temporarily or permanently, reversibly or not. If the effects are temporary and/or reversible, the attack is characterised where action by the adversary is necessary to restore the infrastructure or system (repair of equipment, replacement of a part, reinstallation of a network, etc.).","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",/static/documents/02_international-law-applied-to-operations-in-cyberspace-paper.pdf,"Internally inconsistent provisions within the statement, as well as the use of the term cyberattacks.",
