France,FRA,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Yes,The gravity of a breach of sovereignty will be assessed on a case-by-case basis in accordance with French cyberdefence governance arrangements in order to determine possible responses in compliance with international law.,"International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",/static/documents/02_international-law-applied-to-operations-in-cyberspace-paper.pdf,,
Germany,DEU,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,The legal principle of State sovereignty applies to States’ activities with regard to Cyberspace.,Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"State sovereignty implies, inter alia, that a State retains a right of regulation, enforcement and adjudication (jurisdiction) with regard to both persons engaging in cyber activities and cyber infrastructure on its territory.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,"2(a). Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"Within its borders, a State has the exclusive right – within the framework of international law – to fully exercise its authority, which includes the protection of cyber activities, persons engaging therein as well as cyber infrastructures in the territory of a State against cyber and non-cyber-related interferences attributable to foreign States.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"It is limited only by relevant rules of international law, including international humanitarian law and international human rights law.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"Germany agrees with the view that cyber operations attributable to States which violate the sovereignty of another State are contrary to international law. In this regard, State sovereignty constitutes a legal norm in its own right and may apply directly as a general norm also in cases in which more specific rules applicable to State behaviour, such as the prohibition of intervention or the use of force, are not applicable.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"Germany agrees with the view that cyber operations attributable to States which violate the sovereignty of another State are contrary to international law. In this regard, State sovereignty constitutes a legal norm in its own right and may apply directly as a general norm also in cases in which more specific rules applicable to State behaviour, such as the prohibition of intervention or the use of force, are not applicable.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"Germany essentially concurs with the view proffered, inter alia, in the Tallinn Manual 2.0 that cyber operations attributable to a State which lead to physical effects and harm in the  territory of another State constitute a violation of that State’s territorial sovereignty. This encompasses physical damage to cyber infrastructure components per se and physical effects of such damage on persons or on other infrastructure, i.e. cyber or analogue infrastructure components connected to the damaged cyber component or infrastructure located in the vicinity of the damaged cyber infrastructure (provided a sufficient causal link can be established).",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Yes,"In any case, negligible physical effects and functional impairments below a certain impact threshold cannot – taken by themselves – be deemed to constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Germany,DEU,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"Germany essentially concurs with the view proffered, inter alia, in the Tallinn Manual 2.0 that cyber operations attributable to a State which lead to physical effects and harm in the  territory of another State constitute a violation of that State’s territorial sovereignty. This encompasses physical damage to cyber infrastructure components per se and physical effects of such damage on persons or on other infrastructure, i.e. cyber or analogue infrastructure components connected to the damaged cyber component or infrastructure located in the vicinity of the damaged cyber infrastructure (provided a sufficient causal link can be established).",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,No,"In any case, negligible physical effects and functional impairments below a certain impact threshold cannot – taken by themselves – be deemed to constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"In Germany’s view, this may also apply to certain substantial non-physical (i.e. software-related) functional impairments. In such situations, an evaluation of all relevant circumstances of the individual case will be necessary. If functional impairments result in substantive secondary or indirect physical effects in the territory of the target State (and a sufficient causal link to the cyber operation can be established), a violation of territorial sovereignty will appear highly probable.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,,
Germany,DEU,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,Ambiguous,"In any case, negligible physical effects and functional impairments below a certain impact threshold cannot – taken by themselves – be deemed to constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,7 Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes.,"Germany generally also concurs with the view expressed and discussed in the Tallinn Manual 2.0 that certain effects in form of functional impairments with regard to cyber  infrastructures located in a State’s territory may constitute a violation of a State’s territorial sovereignty. In Germany’s view, this may also apply to certain substantial non-physical (i.e.  software-related) functional impairments. In such situations, an evaluation of all relevant  circumstances of the individual case will be necessary. If functional impairments result in  substantive secondary or indirect physical effects in the territory of the target State (and a  sufficient causal link to the cyber operation can be established), a violation of territorial  sovereignty will appear highly probable.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,No.,"In any case, negligible physical effects and functional impairments below a certain impact threshold cannot – taken by themselves – be deemed to constitute a violation of territorial sovereignty.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"In Germany’s view, this may also apply to certain substantial non-physical (i.e. software-related) functional impairments.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Germany,DEU,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Germany,DEU,9. Must there be proximate causation between the state cyber operation and the effects or interference prohibited by sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous.,"The benchmark for the application of the principle of distinction is the effect caused by a cyber attack, irrespective of whether it is exercised in an offensive or a defensive context. If functional impairments result in substantive secondary or indirect physical effects in the territory of the target State (and a sufficient causal link to the cyber operation can be established), a violation of territorial sovereignty will appear highly probable.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,"Germany references a necessary causal link between some effects and the cyber operation, but does not specify the type or degree of causal connection.",
Germany,DEU,9(a) Must the state cyber operation generate prohibited effects directly?,No.,"If functional impairments result in substantive secondary or indirect physical effects in the territory of the target State (and a sufficient causal link to the cyber operation can be established), a violation of territorial sovereignty will appear highly probable.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,9(b) Do prohibited effects indirectly generated by a state cyber operation constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"If functional impairments result in substantive secondary or indirect physical effects in the territory of the target State (and a sufficient causal link to the cyber operation can be established), a violation of territorial sovereignty will appear highly probable.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,10. Can state cyber operations that deny access to or degrade data in the control of another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Germany,DEU,10(a) Do state sponsored ransomware operations violate the sovereignty of another State?,Silent,,,,,
Germany,DEU,10(b) Can state cyber operations against data residing in cloud servers outside of a State’s territorial jurisdiction constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Germany,DEU,10(c) Does the exercise of one State’s law enforcement tools via cyber means in the networks or systems of another State’s territory violate the sovereignty of the latter State?,Ambiguous,"Germany recognizes that due to the high degree of cross-border interconnectedness of cyber infrastructures, a State’s exercise of its jurisdiction may have unavoidable and immediate repercussions for the cyber infrastructure of other States. While this does not limit a State’s right to exercise its jurisdiction, due regard has to be given to potential adverse effects on third States.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,11. Do State cyber operations that interfere at any level with critical infrastructure in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"Generally, the fact that a piece of critical infrastructure (i.e. infrastructure which plays an indispensable role in ensuring the functioning of the State and its society) or a company of special public interest in the territory of a State has been affected may indicate that a State’s territorial sovereignty has been violated. However, this cannot in and of itself constitute a violation, inter alia because uniform international definitions of the terms do not yet exist.).",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,,
Germany,DEU,12. Can State cyber operations that cause significant national economic impacts for another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Germany,DEU,13. Does cyber espionage by one State in or transiting networks or hardware resident in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Germany,DEU,13(a) Can gaining unauthorized access to computer systems or data within a State’s sovereign control violate the rules of sovereignty?,Silent,,,,,
Germany,DEU,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Yes.,"In Germany’s view, this may also apply to certain substantial non-physical (i.e. software-related) functional impairments. In such situations, an evaluation of all relevant  circumstances of the individual case will be necessary.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,"Germany makes clear that for at least some cyber operations, whether a violation of sovereignty occurs is circumstance dependent.  It is unclear whether this qualification applies only to functionality losses, or more broadly to physical effects as well.",
