Iran,IRN,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Silent,,,,,
Iran,IRN,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes.,"¶2. According to the armed forces of the Islamic Republic of Iran, the territorial sovereignty and jurisdiction of the states are also extended to all elements of the cyberspace.",Position Paper of the Republic of Iran,https://perma.cc/VE54-3YZF,,
Iran,IRN,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes.,"¶1. The Islamic Republic of Iran has developed its sovereignty fields consistent with necessary capabilities for protection of its strategic military, economic, social, cultural, and political authority. In doing so, the development of expertise and advanced cyber tools for active and deterrent cyber-defense is, among others, one of the significant priorities for the protection of the strategic authority of the state.",Position Paper of the Republic of Iran,https://perma.cc/VE54-3YZF,,
Iran,IRN,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Silent,,,,,
Iran,IRN,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Ambiguous,"¶4. Any utilization of cyberspace if and when involves unlawful intrusion to the (public or private) cyber structures which is under the control of another state, may be constituted as the violation of the sovereignty of the targeted state.",Position Paper of the Republic of Iran,https://perma.cc/VE54-3YZF,"Note: The title references “sovereignty policies” and also emphasizes articulation of sovereignty via other rules, so it is not clear if it’s a stand-alone rule, making follow-on question responses all ambiguous as well.",
Iran,IRN,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Ambiguous,"¶4. Any utilization of cyberspace if and when involves unlawful intrusion to the (public or private) cyber structures which is under the control of another state, may be constituted as the violation of the sovereignty of the targeted state.",Position Paper of the Republic of Iran,https://perma.cc/VE54-3YZF,,
Iran,IRN,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Ambiguous,"¶3. Any intentional use of cyber-force with tangible or non-tangible implications which is or can be a threat to the national security or may, due to political, economic, social and cultural destabilization, result in destabilization of national security constitutes a violation of the sovereignty of the state”",Position Paper of the Republic of Iran,https://perma.cc/VE54-3YZF,,
