Ireland,IRL,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,¶4. State sovereignty and international norms and principles that flow from sovereignty apply to the conduct by states of ICT-related activities and to their jurisdiction over ICT infrastructure within their territory.,Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,,
Ireland,IRL,2.  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes.,"¶4. State sovereignty and international norms and principles that flow from sovereignty apply to the conduct by states of ICT-related activities and to their jurisdiction over ICT infrastructure within their territory. The principle of state sovereignty encompasses the concepts of territorial integrity and territorial authority, the independence of state powers, and the equality of states in the international order. A state enjoys a right to exercise jurisdiction in terms of regulation, adjudication and enforcement in relation to cyber infrastructure as well as persons engaging in cyber activities on its territory. A state may also be entitled, in limited circumstances, to exercise extraterritorial jurisdiction in accordance with international law.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,,
Ireland,IRL,"2(a). Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Ambiguous,"¶4. A state enjoys a right to exercise jurisdiction in terms of regulation, adjudication and enforcement in relation to cyber infrastructure as well as persons engaging in cyber activities on its territory.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,,
Ireland,IRL,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes.,"¶34. Ireland considers an open, stable, accessible and safe internet to be an essential foundation for the protection of international human rights law in cyberspace. Any restrictions imposed by states on human rights in a cyber context must fall within parameters recognized as permissible under international human rights law. State sovereignty cannot be relied on as a guise for censoring free internet or otherwise impinging on applicable human rights. Any exercise of state sovereignty must be consistent with international human rights law. ¶7. Sovereignty may not be relied on to justify a state’s non-compliance with applicable obligations under international law. Ireland notes with regret that sovereignty has at times been relied on by some states as justification for cyber measures and/or restrictions within their jurisdiction – such as cyber surveillance or censorship – which compromise human rights, in particular the right to freedom of expression, freedom of thought, conscience and religion, and the right to privacy.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,,
Ireland,IRL,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"¶5. In line with the stated position of many other states, Ireland considers that respect for sovereignty is an obligation in its own right. A violation of state sovereignty by way of cyber activities is capable of amounting to an internationally wrongful act and triggering state responsibility, even if such a violation falls short of the threshold of non-intervention or the use of force.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,,
Ireland,IRL,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"¶5. In line with the stated position of many other states, Ireland considers that respect for sovereignty is an obligation in its own right. A violation of state sovereignty by way of cyber activities is capable of amounting to an internationally wrongful act and triggering state responsibility, even if such a violation falls short of the threshold of non-intervention or the use of force.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,,
Ireland,IRL,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"¶6. A violation of a state’s sovereignty may arise where a cyber-operation attributable to another state causes physical damage to ICT or other infrastructure (whether or not in state ownership or control), functional impairment to such infrastructure, interference with data, and/or secondary effects. The nature and consequences of a cyber-operation are relevant to determining whether a violation has occurred in any given case.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,,
Ireland,IRL,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Silent,,,,,
Ireland,IRL,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Ireland,IRL,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes.,"¶6. A violation of a state’s sovereignty may arise where a cyber-operation attributable to another state causes physical damage to ICT or other infrastructure (whether or not in state ownership or control), functional impairment to such infrastructure, interference with data, and/or secondary effects. The nature and consequences of a cyber-operation are relevant to determining whether a violation has occurred in any given case.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,,
Ireland,IRL,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Ambiguous,"¶6. A violation of a state’s sovereignty may arise where a cyber-operation attributable to another state causes physical damage to ICT or other infrastructure (whether or not in state ownership or control), functional impairment to such infrastructure, interference with data, and/or secondary effects. The nature and consequences of a cyber-operation are relevant to determining whether a violation has occurred in any given case.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,The physical effects are considered on a case-by-case basis which is why this would be an ambiguous standard.,
Ireland,IRL,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"¶6. A violation of a state’s sovereignty may arise where a cyber-operation attributable to another state causes physical damage to ICT or other infrastructure (whether or not in state ownership or control), functional impairment to such infrastructure, interference with data, and/or secondary effects. The nature and consequences of a cyber-operation are relevant to determining whether a violation has occurred in any given case.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,,
