Israel,ISR,1.  Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,¶3. The first is that sovereignty is a cornerstone of international law and international relations.,Israel’s Perspective on Key Legal and Practical Issues Concerning the Application of International Law to Cyber Operations,https://perma.cc/3ENJ-5H3N,,
Israel,ISR,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Silent,,,,"Note: Israel is firm that States have sovereign interests to protecting cyber infrastructure and activity on its territory, but it is not clear if that interest constitutes authority or a right",
Israel,ISR,"2(a). Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Israel,ISR,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Silent,,,,,
Israel,ISR,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Ambiguous,"¶2. To begin with, there are diverging views regarding whether sovereignty is merely a principle, from which legal rules are derived, or a binding rule of international law in itself, the violation of which could be considered an internationally wrongful act.  This issue has many facets, and while I will not offer any definitive position for the time being, I would like to stress a number of important points ¶3. The first is that sovereignty is a cornerstone of international law and international relations. Of course, we need to distinguish, in this regard, between sovereignty, which is typically used as a general concept that connotes  independence, and “territorial sovereignty,” which is an international legal rule. States will sometimes point to the need to protect their sovereignty,  referring broadly to their political will and autonomy, without necessarily referring to a legal rule. The two meanings are sometimes conflated, and we  need to be very careful when drawing legal conclusions. ¶5. In practice, States occasionally do conduct cyber activities that transit through, and target, networks and computers located in other  States, for example for national defense, cybersecurity, or law enforcement  purposes. Under existing international law, it is not clear whether these types  of actions are violations of the rule of territorial sovereignty, or perhaps that  our understanding of territorial sovereignty in cyberspace is substantively different from its meaning in the physical world.",Israel’s Perspective on Key Legal and Practical Issues Concerning the Application of International Law to Cyber Operations,https://perma.cc/3ENJ-5H3N,"Does say territorial sovereignty is an international legal rule, but doesn’t take a position on whether that translates into a rule for cyberspace",
Israel,ISR,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Ambiguous,"¶11. The fact that a cyber operation is not an attack does not mean that has no legal limitations apply thereto ¶5. Under existing international law, it is not clear whether these types of actions are violations of the rule of territorial sovereignty, or perhaps that our understanding of territorial sovereignty in cyberspace is substantively different from its meaning in the physical world.",Israel’s Perspective on Key Legal and Practical Issues Concerning the Application of International Law to Cyber Operations,https://perma.cc/3ENJ-5H3N,Does not directly pertain to violations on sovereignty,
