Italy,ITA,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"Italy attaches fundamental importance to the application of the principle of sovereignty to cyberspace, including its ancillary rules, such as the right to internal self-determination. Italy considers that both the internal and external aspects of sovereignty apply in cyberspace. The principle of sovereignty is a primary rule of international law, the violation of which amounts to an internationally wrongful act.",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/XA4M-YQH9,,
Italy,ITA,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"Each State’s exclusive jurisdiction over the physical, social and logical layers of cyberspace located on its territory may be exercised within the limits imposed by international law, including international obligations deriving from diplomatic privileges and immunities and those arising from human rights obligations",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/XA4M-YQH9,,
Italy,ITA,"2(a). Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Italy,ITA,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"Each State’s exclusive jurisdiction over the physical, social and logical layers of cyberspace located on its territory may be exercised within the limits imposed by international law, including international obligations deriving from diplomatic privileges and immunities and those arising from human rights obligations.",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/XA4M-YQH9,,
Italy,ITA,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"The principle of sovereignty is a primary rule of international law, the violation of which amounts to an internationally wrongful act.",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/XA4M-YQH9,,
Italy,ITA,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"The principle of sovereignty is a primary rule of international law, the violation of which amounts to an internationally wrongful act.",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/XA4M-YQH9,,
Italy,ITA,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Silent,,,,,
Italy,ITA,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Silent,,,,,
Italy,ITA,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Yes,"Italy considers that the principle in question prohibits a State from conducting cyber operations, which produce harmful effects on the territory of another State, irrespective of the physical location of the perpetrator.",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/XA4M-YQH9,,
Italy,ITA,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"Italy considers that the principle in question prohibits a State from conducting cyber operations, which produce harmful effects on the territory of another State, irrespective of the physical location of the perpetrator.",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/XA4M-YQH9,Harmful is undefined so it could (or could not) be a reference to physical effects,
