Japan,JPN,1.  Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"¶4.... [I[t can be presumed that, in some cases, a violation of sovereignty constitutes a violation of international law even when it does not fall within the scope of unlawful intervention.",Basic Position of the Government of Japan on International Law Applicable to Cyber Operations,https://perma.cc/9V4X-62EY,An affirmative answer can be inferred from the Statement.,
Japan,JPN,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Silent,,,,,
Japan,JPN,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Japan,JPN,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"¶1. Pursuant to international human rights law, States are under the obligation to respect human rights. The human rights that must be respected in cyberspace include all human rights that are recognized under international human rights law, such as civil, political, economic, social and cultural rights. The human rights that are particularly relevant in the context of cyberspace include the right to privacy, freedom of thought and conscience, freedom of expression, and guarantee of due process",Basic Position of the Government of Japan on International Law Applicable to Cyber Operations,https://perma.cc/9V4X-62EY,,
Japan,JPN,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"¶3. In the case of cyber operations as well, there is an internationally wrongful act when a State violates primary rules, including the principles of sovereignty, non-intervention, prohibition of the use of force",Basic Position of the Government of Japan on International Law Applicable to Cyber Operations,https://perma.cc/9V4X-62EY,,
Japan,JPN,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"¶4.… it can be presumed that, in some cases, a violation of sovereignty constitutes a violation of international law even when it does not fall within the scope of unlawful intervention.",Basic Position of the Government of Japan on International Law Applicable to Cyber Operations,https://perma.cc/9V4X-62EY,There seems to be precedent set that there are instances where certain actions can still fall under the scope of illegality in international law,
Japan,JPN,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Ambiguous,"¶5. An act of causing physical damage or loss of functionality by means of cyber operations against critical infrastructure, including medical institutions, may constitute an unlawful intervention, depending on the circumstances, and at any rate, it may constitute a violation of sovereignty.",Basic Position of the Government of Japan on International Law Applicable to Cyber Operations,https://perma.cc/9V4X-62EY,,
Japan,JPN,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Silent,,,,,
