The Republic of Korea,,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"“Every State is independent in its international relations and possesses the supreme authority within its territory. Sovereignty must be equally and mutually respected among States, and such sovereignty is equally recognized in cyberspace...As a logical consequence of the principle of sovereign equality, no State may intervene in the domestic affairs of another. This principle is explicitly codified in Article 2(7) of the UN Charter and has been affirmed in international jurisprudence. This principle applies equally in cyberspace.”",National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/P3W7-9URM,,
The Republic of Korea,,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"“Based on the principle of territorial jurisdiction, a State exercises authority over  cyber infrastructure located within its territory, over individuals engaging in  cyber activities from within its borders, and over the cyber activities themselves.”

FN 1. Cyberspace is not a virtual realm existing independently of physical components. Rather, it is constructed upon concrete physical infrastructure, such as computer network systems, fiber-optic cables, and satellite  systems, thereby making cyberspace subject to territorial jurisdiction and sovereign authority. See also 2015 GGE Report, para. 28(a): “States have jurisdiction over the ICT infrastructure located within their territory.”",National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/P3W7-9URM,,
The Republic of Korea,,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"“Based on the principle of territorial jurisdiction, a State exercises authority over cyber infrastructure located within its territory, over individuals engaging in  cyber activities from within its borders, and over the cyber activities themselves.”

FN 1. Cyberspace is not a virtual realm existing independently of physical components. Rather, it is constructed upon concrete physical infrastructure, such as computer network systems, fiber-optic cables, and satellite  systems, thereby making cyberspace subject to territorial jurisdiction and sovereign authority. See also 2015 GGE Report, para. 28(a): “States have jurisdiction over the ICT infrastructure located within their territory.”",National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/P3W7-9URM,,
The Republic of Korea,,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"§1.“The Republic of Korea holds the view that the United Nations Charter, together with other generally accepted international norms and customary international law, applies equally to activities in cyberspace.” §5.“The Republic of Korea affirms that international human rights law applies online in the same manner as it does offline... Fundamental rights—including the right to privacy, freedom of expression, access to information, protection against discrimination, and the prevention of hate speech—must be protected and upheld in cyberspace for all individuals, …”",National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/P3W7-9URM,,
