Netherlands,NLD,10(c) Does the exercise of one State’s law enforcement tools via cyber means in the networks or systems of another State’s territory violate the sovereignty of the latter State?,Ambiguous,The act of exercising investigative powers in a cross-border context is traditionally deemed a violation of a country’s sovereignty unless the country in question has explicitly granted permission (by means of a treaty or other instrument). Opinion is divided as to what qualifies as exercising investigative powers in a cross-border context and when it is permissible without a legal basis founded in a treaty.,"Government of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, Appendix: International law in cyberspace",https://perma.cc/YFD4-LB25,Update to the actual Dutch position(s)... GPC:  Read full quote on this (cited above).,
Netherlands,NLD,11. Do State cyber operations that interfere at any level with critical infrastructure in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
