New Zealand,NZL,1.  Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"¶11. The principle of sovereignty prohibits the interference by one state in the inherently governmental functions of another and prohibits the exercise of state power or authority on the territory of another state. In the physical realm, the principle has legal effect through the prohibition on the use of force, through the rule of non-intervention and also through a standalone rule of territorial sovereignty. Subject to limited exceptions (e.g. authorisation by the United Nations Security Council, self-defence, consent), that standalone rule prohibits a state from sending its troops or police forces into or through, or its aircraft over, foreign territory, and prohibits a state from carrying out official investigations or otherwise exercising jurisdiction on foreign territory.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Silent,,,,,
New Zealand,NZL,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Ambiguous,"¶14. New Zealand considers that the rule of territorial sovereignty as applied in the cyber context does not prohibit states from taking necessary measures, with minimally destructive effects, to defend against the harmful activity of malicious cyber actors.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"¶29. International human rights law applies to cyber activities. States must comply with their obligations to protect and respect human rights online, including the right to freedom of expression and the right not to be subjected to arbitrary and unlawful interference with privacy. States are obliged to respect and ensure human rights to those individuals within their territory and subject to their jurisdiction. The circumstances in which states exercise jurisdiction, through cyber means, over individuals outside their territory is currently unsettled and would benefit from further discussion in multilateral fora.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,¶12. New Zealand considers that the standalone rule of territorial sovereignty also applies in the cyber context but acknowledges that further state practice is required for the precise boundaries of its application to crystallize,The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"¶15. A detailed factual enquiry is required in each case to determine whether state cyber activity that has effects manifesting on the territory of another state, but which does not amount to a use of force or a prohibited intervention, nonetheless involves a violation of the standalone rule of territorial sovereignty",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Silent,,,,,
New Zealand,NZL,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Yes,"¶14. Bearing those factors in mind, and having regard to developing state practice, New Zealand considers that territorial sovereignty prohibits states from using cyber means to cause significant harmful effects manifesting on the territory of another state. However, New Zealand does not consider that territorial sovereignty prohibits every unauthorised intrusion into a foreign ICT system or prohibits all cyber activity which has effects on the territory of another state.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Ambiguous,"¶11. Subject to limited exceptions (e.g. authorisation by the United Nations Security Council, self-defence, consent), that standalone rule prohibits a state from sending its troops or police forces into or through, or its aircraft over, foreign territory, and prohibits a state from carrying out official investigations or otherwise exercising jurisdiction on foreign territory.  ¶11. A detailed factual enquiry is required in each case to determine whether state cyber activity that has effects manifesting on the territory of another state, but which does not amount to a use of force or a prohibited intervention, nonetheless involves a violation of the standalone rule of territorial sovereignty. That factual enquiry should take into account the scale and significance of the effects, the objective of the activity, and the nature of the target.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶14. New Zealand considers that territorial sovereignty prohibits states from using cyber means to cause significant harmful effects manifesting on the territory of another state.  ¶15. A detailed factual enquiry is required in each case to determine whether state cyber activity that has effects manifesting on the territory of another state, but which does not amount to a use of force or a prohibited intervention, nonetheless involves a violation of the standalone rule of territorial sovereignty. That factual enquiry should take into account the scale and significance of the effects, the objective of the activity, and the nature of the target.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,No,"¶14. New Zealand does not consider that territorial sovereignty prohibits every unauthorised intrusion into a foreign ICT system or prohibits all cyber activity which has effects on the territory of another state. There is a range of circumstances – in addition to pure espionage activity – in which an unauthorised cyber intrusion, including one causing effects on the territory of another state, would not be internationally wrongful.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",Ambiguous,"¶14. Bearing those factors in mind, and having regard to developing state practice, New Zealand considers that territorial sovereignty prohibits states from using cyber means to cause significant harmful effects manifesting on the territory of another state",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,,
New Zealand,NZL,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,Ambiguous,"¶14. Bearing those factors in mind, and having regard to developing state practice, New Zealand considers that territorial sovereignty prohibits states from using cyber means to cause significant harmful effects manifesting on the territory of another state",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,7 Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"¶14. New Zealand does not consider that territorial sovereignty prohibits every unauthorised intrusion into a foreign ICT system or prohibits all cyber activity which has effects on the territory of another state. There is a range of circumstances – in addition to pure espionage activity – in which an unauthorised cyber intrusion, including one causing effects on the territory of another state, would not be internationally wrongful. For example, New Zealand considers that the rule of territorial sovereignty as applied in the cyber context does not prohibit states from taking necessary measures, with minimally destructive effects, to defend against the harmful activity of malicious cyber actors.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"¶14. New Zealand considers that the rule of territorial sovereignty as applied in the cyber context does not prohibit states from taking necessary measures, with minimally destructive effects, to defend against the harmful activity of malicious cyber actors.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"¶14. Bearing those factors in mind, and having regard to developing state practice, New Zealand considers that territorial sovereignty prohibits states from using cyber means to cause significant harmful effects manifesting on the territory of another state",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
