New Zealand,NZL,13. Does cyber espionage by one State in or transiting networks or hardware resident in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,No,"¶14. New Zealand does not consider that territorial sovereignty prohibits every unauthorised intrusion into a foreign ICT system or prohibits all cyber activity which has effects on the territory of another state. There is a range of circumstances – in addition to pure espionage activity – in which an unauthorised cyber intrusion, including one causing effects on the territory of another state, would not be internationally wrongful.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,13a. Can gaining unauthorized access to computer systems or data within a State’s sovereign control violate the rules of sovereignty?,Ambiguous,¶14. New Zealand does not consider that territorial sovereignty prohibits every unauthorised intrusion into a foreign ICT system or prohibits all cyber activity which has effects on the territory of another state.,The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
New Zealand,NZL,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Yes,"¶15. A detailed factual enquiry is required in each case to determine whether state cyber activity that has effects manifesting on the territory of another state, but which does not amount to a use of force or a prohibited intervention, nonetheless involves a violation of the standalone rule of territorial sovereignty. That factual enquiry should take into account the scale and significance of the effects, the objective of the activity, and the nature of the target.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5JRG-RPLW,,
Norway,NOR,1. Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"Sovereignty is not just a principle, but also a primary rule of international law. The principle of sovereignty is one of the fundamental principles of international law and applies in cyberspace.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Norway,NOR,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"The internal dimension of a State’s sovereignty includes the exclusive right to exercise jurisdiction within its territory, including over the information systems located on its territory, and to exercise independent State powers. The external dimension includes the right of the State to decide its foreign policy and to enter into international agreements. Both dimensions of sovereignty apply in cyberspace, subject only to obligations under international law.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Norway,NOR,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Norway,NOR,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"States must comply with their human rights obligations in cyberspace, just as they must in the physical world. States must both respect and protect human rights.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Norway,NOR,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"Norway is of the view that sovereignty constitutes both an international law principle from which various rules derive, such as the prohibition of intervention and the prohibition of the use of force, and a primary rule in its own right capable of being violated.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Norway,NOR,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"Thus, cyber operations that do not amount to a prohibited intervention or a prohibited use of force may nevertheless amount to a violation of a State’s sovereignty under international law.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Norway,NOR,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"A cyber operation that manifests itself on another State’s territory may, depending on its nature, the scale of the intrusion and its consequences, constitute a violation of sovereignty. . . . Similarly, a cyber operation that interferes with or usurps the inherently governmental functions of another State may constitute a violation of sovereignty. . . The precise threshold of what constitute a cyber operation in violation of sovereignty is not settled in international law, and will depend on a case-by-case assessment.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Norway,NOR,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Ambiguous,"The precise threshold of what constitute a cyber operation in violation of sovereignty is not settled in international law, and will depend on a case-by-case assessment.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/a8911fc020c94eb386a1ec7917bf0d03/norwegian_positions.pdf,,
Norway,NOR,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Norway,NOR,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,Causing physical damage by cyber means on another State’s territory may easily qualify as a violation of territorial sovereignty.,Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Norway,NOR,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Ambiguous,"Causing physical damage by cyber means on another State’s territory may easily qualify as a violation of territorial sovereignty. . .  . The precise threshold of what constitute a cyber operation in violation of sovereignty is not settled in international law, and will depend on a case-by-case assessment.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Norway,NOR,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"Causing physical damage by cyber means on another State’s territory may easily qualify as a violation of territorial sovereignty. For example, a cyber operation against an industrial control system at a petrochemical plant that led to a malfunction and a subsequent fire would constitute a violation of the State’s territorial sovereignty. In addition to physical damage, causing cyber infrastructure to lose functionality may also be taken into consideration and may amount to a violation.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Norway,NOR,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,GPC:  The question is very specific, I’m inclined to go with silent.
Norway,NOR,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,No,"Causing physical damage by cyber means on another State’s territory may easily qualify as a violation of territorial sovereignty. . .  . In addition to physical damage, causing cyber infrastructure to lose functionality may also be taken into consideration and may amount to a violation. This includes the use of crypto viruses to encrypt data and thus render them unusable for a substantial period of time...The precise threshold of what constitute a cyber operation in violation of sovereignty is not settled in international law, and will depend on a case-by-case assessment.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,"While significant physical effects are more likely to be considered violations, it's not necessarily limited to only those",
Norway,NOR,7 Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"Causing physical damage by cyber means on another State’s territory may easily qualify as a violation of territorial sovereignty. For example, a cyber operation against an industrial control system at a petrochemical plant that led to a malfunction and a subsequent fire would constitute a violation of the State’s territorial sovereignty. In addition to physical damage, causing cyber infrastructure to lose functionality may also be taken into consideration and may amount to a violation. This includes the use of crypto viruses to encrypt data and thus render them unusable for a substantial period of time.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Norway,NOR,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,No,"The precise threshold of what constitute a cyber operation in violation of sovereignty is not settled in international law, and will depend on a case-by-case assessment.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Norway,NOR,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Norway,NOR,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Norway,NOR,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"Similarly, a cyber operation that interferes with or usurps the inherently governmental functions of another State may constitute a violation of sovereignty.  Accordingly, what matters is not whether physical damage, injury, or loss of functionality has resulted, but whether the cyber operation has interfered with data or services that are necessary for the exercise of inherently governmental functions.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Norway,NOR,9. Must there be proximate causation between the state cyber operation and the effects or interference prohibited by sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Norway,NOR,9(a) Must the state cyber operation generate prohibited effects directly?,Silent,,,,,
Norway,NOR,9(b) Do prohibited effects indirectly generated by a state cyber operation constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Norway,NOR,10. Can state cyber operations that deny access to or degrade data in the control of another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"Similarly, a cyber operation that interferes with or usurps the inherently governmental functions of another State may constitute a violation of sovereignty. This is based on the premise that a State enjoys the exclusive right to exercise within its territory, to the exclusion of any other State, the functions of a State’. Accordingly, what matters is not whether physical damage, injury, or loss of functionality has resulted, but whether the cyber operation has interfered with data or services that are necessary for the exercise of inherently governmental functions.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Norway,NOR,10(a) Do state sponsored ransomware operations violate the sovereignty of another State?,Ambiguous,"In addition to physical damage, causing cyber infrastructure to lose functionality may also be taken into consideration and may amount to a violation. This includes the use of crypto viruses to encrypt data and thus render them unusable for a substantial period of time. The principle of sovereignty encompasses cyber infrastructure located in a State’s territory irrespective of whether it is governmental or private.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Norway,NOR,10(b) Can state cyber operations against data residing in cloud servers outside of a State’s territorial jurisdiction constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,No mention of the location of the data exists.,
Norway,NOR,10(c) Does the exercise of one State’s law enforcement tools via cyber means in the networks or systems of another State’s territory violate the sovereignty of the latter State?,Silent,,,,,
Norway,NOR,11. Do State cyber operations that interfere at any level with critical infrastructure in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,I would move this to silent.  The question is very specific, looking for whether approach is different for CI.
Norway,NOR,12. Can State cyber operations that cause significant national economic impacts for another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Norway,NOR,13. Does cyber espionage by one State in or transiting networks or hardware resident in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Norway,NOR,13a. Can gaining unauthorized access to computer systems or data within a State’s sovereign control violate the rules of sovereignty?,Silent,,,,,
Norway,NOR,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Yes,"The precise threshold of what constitute a cyber operation in violation of sovereignty is not settled in international law, and will depend on a case-by-case assessment.",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
