Poland,POL,1.  Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,State sovereignty is a basic principle of international law.,"The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §2 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,,
Poland,POL,2.  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"State sovereignty is a basic principle of international law. According to this principle, states are independent and equal in international relations, while their territorial integrity and political independence are inviolable. As a consequence, states exercise supreme power over their own territory.","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §2 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,Poland’s position confers supreme power over a state’s own territory based on sovereignty and territorial integrity.,
Poland,POL,"2(a) Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"A state exercises power over cyberspace users located within its territory, over IT infrastructure and over data. While respecting the norms of international law by which it is bound, it may exercise its sovereign prerogatives over such actors and facilities. It is also entitled to protect them.","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §2 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,"Poland’s position recognizes that sovereignty includes prerogatives over actors, facilities, and users of information technology infrastructure. The right to protect cyber infrastructure is extensive enough to include safeguarding cyber activity located on a state’s own territory.",
Poland,POL,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,Respecting sovereignty in cyberspace must not serve as an excuse for violations of international human rights law.,"The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 6, §7, (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,,
Poland,POL,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,. . .  Actions in cyberspace that violate the prohibition of the use of force and the principle of non-intervention in affairs falling under the domestic jurisdiction of a state would also violate the principle of sovereignty . . . A cyberattack that does not reach the threshold of the prohibited use of force may be deemed a prohibited intervention or an action that violates the principle of sovereignty.,"The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §2; pg. 5, §4 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,"Poland’s position indicates that violations of sovereignty also exist independently from any prohibited acts, although one act may overlap in terms of violations of all the applicable principle of international law.",
Poland,POL,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,A cyberattack that does not reach the threshold of the prohibited use of force may be deemed a prohibited intervention or an action that violates the principle of sovereignty.,"The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, §4 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,Poland’s position indicates that violations of sovereignty also exist independently from any prohibited acts.,
Poland,POL,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"Poland is of the opinion that in the event of a hostile operation conducted in cyberspace, causing serious adverse effects within the territory of a state, such actions should be considered a violation of the principle of sovereignty, irrespective of whether such effects are of kinetic nature or are limited to cyberspace. The violation of the principle of sovereignty may be exemplified by a conduct attributable to a third country that consists in interfering with the functioning of state organs, for instance by preventing the proper functioning of ICT networks, services or systems of public entities, or by a theft, erasure or public disclosure of data belonging to such entities","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §2 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,Elements: Hostile Cyberspace Operation  Serious Adverse Effects Within Territory of a State Does not matter if effects are physical or cyberspace,
Poland,POL,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Ambiguous,"Poland is of the opinion that in the event of a hostile operation conducted in cyberspace, causing serious adverse effects within the territory of a state, such actions should be considered a violation of the principle of sovereignty, irrespective of whether such effects are of kinetic nature or are limited to cyberspace. The violation of the principle of sovereignty may be exemplified by a conduct attributable to a third country that consists in interfering with the functioning of state organs, for instance by preventing the proper functioning of ICT networks, services or systems of public entities, or by a theft, erasure or public disclosure of data belonging to such entities","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §2 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,Inferred from statement,
Poland,POL,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Poland,POL,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"Poland is of the opinion that in the event of a hostile operation conducted in cyberspace, causing serious adverse effects within the territory of a state, such actions should be considered a violation of the principle of sovereignty, irrespective of whether such effects are of kinetic nature or are limited to cyberspace.","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §2 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,,
Poland,POL,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Ambiguous,"Poland is of the opinion that in the event of a hostile operation conducted in cyberspace, causing serious adverse effects within the territory of a state, such actions should be considered a violation of the principle of sovereignty, irrespective of whether such effects are of kinetic nature or are limited to cyberspace.",,,Inferred from statement re serious adverse effects,
Poland,POL,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"Poland is of the opinion that in the event of a hostile operation conducted in cyberspace, causing serious adverse effects within the territory of a state, such actions should be considered a violation of the principle of sovereignty, irrespective of whether such effects are of kinetic nature or are limited to cyberspace.","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §2 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,,
Poland,POL,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,.,,,,
Poland,POL,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,No,"Poland is of the opinion that in the event of a hostile operation conducted in cyberspace, causing serious adverse effects within the territory of a state, such actions should be considered a violation of the principle of sovereignty, irrespective of whether such effects are of kinetic nature or are limited to cyberspace.","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §2 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,,
Poland,POL,7 Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"Poland is of the opinion that in the event of a hostile operation conducted in cyberspace, causing serious adverse effects within the territory of a state, such actions should be considered a violation of the principle of sovereignty, irrespective of whether such effects are of kinetic nature or are limited to cyberspace. The violation of the principle of sovereignty may be exemplified by a conduct attributable to a third country that consists in interfering with the functioning of state organs, for instance by preventing the proper functioning of ICT networks, services or systems of public entities, or by a theft, erasure or public disclosure of data belonging to such entities.","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §2 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,,
Poland,POL,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,No,"Poland is of the opinion that in the event of a hostile operation conducted in cyberspace, causing serious adverse effects within the territory of a state, such actions should be considered a violation of the principle of sovereignty, irrespective of whether such effects are of kinetic nature or are limited to cyberspace.","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §2 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,See “serious”,
Poland,POL,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Yes,"Poland is of the opinion that in the event of a hostile operation conducted in cyberspace, causing serious adverse effects within the territory of a state, such actions should be considered a violation of the principle of sovereignty, irrespective of whether such effects are of kinetic nature or are limited to cyberspace.","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §2 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,,
Poland,POL,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Poland,POL,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"The violation of the principle of sovereignty may be exemplified by a conduct attributable to a third country that consists in interfering with the functioning of state organs, for instance by preventing the proper functioning of ICT networks, services or systems of public entities, or by a theft, erasure or public disclosure of data belonging to such entities.","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, §2 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,.,
