Romania,ROU,1.  Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,Romania considers that respect for the state sovereignty is an international obligation per se . . .,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,By inference,
Romania,ROU,2.  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"States have an obligation to respect the sovereignty of other States and refrain from activities that constitute a violation of their sovereignty; this holds true both in what concerns the internal as well as the external facet of the principle of sovereignty. International law does not recognise a right to States to derogate from their  international human rights obligations as a defensive-type measure – for instance to  restrict access to internet in all circumstance as a responsive measure to counter some  types of conduct in cyberspace (which generally pertain to criminal law, like: countering terrorism, violent extremism or fraud).",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Romania,ROU,"2(a). Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Romania,ROU,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,whatever regulation a State adopts (by virtue of its sovereign right) it must conform with its international obligations in the field of human rights.,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Romania,ROU,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"Romania considers that respect for the state sovereignty is an international obligation per se, the breach of which constitutes an internationally wrongful act; States have an obligation to respect the sovereignty of other States and refrain from activities that constitute a violation of their sovereignty; this holds true both in what concerns the internal as well as the external facet of the principle of sovereignty.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Romania,ROU,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"Romania considers that respect for the state sovereignty is an international obligation per se, the breach of which constitutes an internationally wrongful act; States have an obligation to respect the sovereignty of other States and refrain from activities that constitute a violation of their sovereignty; this holds true both in what concerns the internal as well as the external facet of the principle of sovereignty.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Romania,ROU,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"In relation to these aspects, RO is of the view that cyber operations (conducted by a State organ or by a person or entity exercising elements of governmental authority or by a person acting under the instructions of or under the direction or control of a State) that interferes with or prevents in any way a State from exercising its (internal and/ or external) sovereign prerogatives (i.e. authority over its territory, over the property and persons situated therein) constitute a violation of the principle of State sovereignty and, thus, a breach of international law.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,Elements Cyber Operation Interferes with or prevents in any way a State from exercising its sovereign prerogatives,
Romania,ROU,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Ambiguous,"In relation to these aspects, RO is of the view that cyber operations (conducted by a State organ or by a person or entity exercising elements of governmental authority or by a person acting under the instructions of or under the direction or control of a State) that interferes with or prevents in any way a State from exercising its (internal and/ or external) sovereign prerogatives (i.e. authority over its territory, over the property and persons situated therein) constitute a violation of the principle of State sovereignty and, thus, a breach of international law",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,"w/respect to “any interference w/exercise of sov. authority, but not clear if de minimis applies to physical or functionality effects",
