Singapore,SGP,1.  Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"5. Singapore affirms that the following key principles enshrined in the UN Charter apply in cyberspace as they do in the physical world, and are of fundamental importance to small States, such as Singapore:  First, the principles of State sovereignty and sovereign equality of all States. Singapore’s position is that a cyber operation could, in certain circumstances, amount to a violation of sovereignty.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Singapore,SGP,2.  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"Yes as elaborated in Statement delivered at OEWG 4th Substantive Session (6-10 Mar 2023)  and   Intervention on International Law for the UN OEWG Informal Intersessional Meetings, 24 May 2023 Both accessible from  Open-ended working group on information and communication technologies (2021) | United Nations “On the application of sovereignty, Singapore’s position is that the territorial sovereignty of a State extends over cyber-infrastructure located in its territory and over activities associated with such infrastructure.”",,,,
Singapore,SGP,"2(a). Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,,,,,
Singapore,SGP,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Ambiguous,18. The same human rights which apply offline also apply online.,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Singapore,SGP,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Ambiguous,"5. Singapore affirms that the following key principles enshrined in the UN Charter apply in cyberspace as they do in the physical world, and are of fundamental importance to small States, such as Singapore:  First, the principles of State sovereignty and sovereign equality of all States. Singapore’s position is that a cyber operation could, in certain circumstances, amount to a violation of sovereignty.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Singapore,SGP,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Ambiguous,"First, the principles of State sovereignty and sovereign equality of all States. Singapore’s position is that a cyber operation could, in certain circumstances, amount to a violation of sovereignty.  Second, and as a corollary of the first, the principle of non-intervention in each other’s internal affairs. Singapore affirms that the principle of non-intervention in the internal affairs of other States applies to cyberspace.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,"Singapore indicates that sovereignty is a standalone principle, whereas the principle of non-intervention is a logical consequence of sovereignty. However, malicious cyber activities rising to the level of internationally wrongful acts are mainly discussed in terms of attribution, counter-measures, and peaceful dispute resolution, rather than violations of sovereignty.",
Singapore,SGP,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,No,,,,,
Singapore,SGP,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Silent,,,,,
Singapore,SGP,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,"Singapore does not explicitly discuss unauthorized cyber operations or consent as a criterion of sovereignty violations, although these considerations may play a role in determining whether a state’s government was coerced in its exercise of sovereign prerogatives.",
