Sweden,SWE,1.  Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,State sovereignty provides a basic foundation for other principles and rules such as those governing the prohibition of intervention and the prohibition of the use of force.,Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,,
Sweden,SWE,2.  Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"The principle of sovereign equality of States is also applicable to cyberspace. Within their territories, States have jurisdiction and the right to exercise authority within the framework of international law... A State’s jurisdiction and authority apply to persons and objects within its territorial borders, including cyber-related activities. A State has a right to protect persons and objects within its territory, or otherwise under its jurisdiction, against interference by cyber means.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,,
Sweden,SWE,"2(a). Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"A State’s jurisdiction and authority apply to persons and objects within its territorial borders, including cyber-related activities. A State has a right to protect persons and objects within its territory, or otherwise under its jurisdiction, against interference by cyber means.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,,
Sweden,SWE,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"Human rights apply online as they do offline... The same human rights responsibilities and obligations that States have in the physical world also apply in the digital world. Although human rights are universal and indivisible, some are particularly relevant to the use of the internet, including (but not limited to) freedom of opinion, expression and information, freedom of association and assembly, and privacy. To enable the full enjoyment of human rights online, it is crucial that the internet remains open, free and secure with equal access and inclusiveness for all.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,,
Sweden,SWE,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"State sovereignty provides a basic foundation for other principles and rules such as those governing the prohibition of intervention and the prohibition of the use of force. However, States also have an obligation to respect the sovereignty of other States, and a breach of this obligation would amount to a wrongful act and give rise to State responsibility.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,,
Sweden,SWE,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"However, States also have an obligation to respect the sovereignty of other States, and a breach of this obligation would amount to a wrongful act and give rise to State responsibility ...",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,"Sweden’s position recognizes that cyber operations resulting in damage or loss of functionality may lead to a sovereignty violation. Given the separate treatment of sovereignty and non-intervention, a standalone violation of sovereignty that is not a prohibited intervention still constitutes a wrongful act.",
Sweden,SWE,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"Altering and interfering with data without causing physical harm may also violate sovereignty. Such acts include those directed against cyber infrastructure belonging to private individuals or entities. Interference with a State’s inherently governmental functions may also constitute a violation of State sovereignty, including when undertaken with cyber means. Whether an intrusion has in fact resulted in a violation of sovereignty needs to be assessed on a case-by-case basis taking into consideration the nature and character of the intrusion.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,,
Sweden,SWE,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Silent,,,,,
Sweden,SWE,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Sweden,SWE,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"In general, Sweden is of the view that violations of sovereignty may arise from cyber operations that result in damage or loss of functionality. Altering and interfering with data without causing physical harm may also violate sovereignty.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,,
Sweden,SWE,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Ambiguous,"In general, Sweden is of the view that violations of sovereignty may arise from cyber operations that result in damage or loss of functionality . . . Whether an intrusion has in fact resulted in a violation of sovereignty needs to be assessed on a case-by-case basis taking into consideration the nature and character of the intrusion.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,,
Sweden,SWE,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"In general, Sweden is of the view that violations of sovereignty may arise from cyber operations that result in damage or loss of functionality. Altering and interfering with data without causing physical harm may also violate sovereignty.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,The first sentence in the excerpt of Sweden’s position suggests that either physical damage or loss of functionality includes altering and interfering with data without causing physical harm.,
Sweden,SWE,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,,
Sweden,SWE,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,Silent,.,,,.,
Sweden,SWE,7 Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"In general, Sweden is of the view that violations of sovereignty may arise from cyber operations that result in damage or loss of functionality. Altering and interfering with data without causing physical harm may also violate sovereignty . . . Whether an intrusion has in fact resulted in a violation of sovereignty needs to be assessed on a case-by-case basis taking into consideration the nature and character of the intrusion.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,"If cyber operations result in any damage or loss of functionality, then a sovereignty violation may occur, according to Sweden’s position.",
Sweden,SWE,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"In general, Sweden is of the view that violations of sovereignty may arise from cyber operations that result in damage or loss of functionality. Altering and interfering with data without causing physical harm may also violate sovereignty . . . Whether an intrusion has in fact resulted in a violation of sovereignty needs to be assessed on a case-by-case basis taking into consideration the nature and character of the intrusion.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,,
Sweden,SWE,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Ambiguous,"In general, Sweden is of the view that violations of sovereignty may arise from cyber operations that result in damage or loss of functionality. Altering and interfering with data without causing physical harm may also violate sovereignty . . . Whether an intrusion has in fact resulted in a violation of sovereignty needs to be assessed on a case-by-case basis taking into consideration the nature and character of the intrusion.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,"If cyber operations result in any damage or loss of functionality, then a sovereignty violation may occur, according to Sweden’s position. The qualification of functionality losses as significant may be a consideration of the intrusion's nature and character.",
Sweden,SWE,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Sweden,SWE,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"Interference with a State’s inherently governmental functions may also constitute a violation of State sovereignty, including when undertaken with cyber means. Whether an intrusion has in fact resulted in a violation of sovereignty needs to be assessed on a case-by-case basis taking into consideration the nature and character of the intrusion.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,"If cyber operations interfere with a state’s inherently governmental functions, then a sovereignty violation may occur, according to Sweden’s position.",
Sweden,SWE,9. Must there be proximate causation between the state cyber operation and the effects or interference prohibited by sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Sweden,SWE,9(a) Must the state cyber operation generate prohibited effects directly?,Silent,,,,,
Sweden,SWE,9(b) Do prohibited effects indirectly generated by a state cyber operation constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Sweden,SWE,10. Can state cyber operations that deny access to or degrade data in the control of another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"In general, Sweden is of the view that violations of sovereignty may arise from cyber operations that result in damage or loss of functionality Altering and interfering with data without causing physical harm may also violate sovereignty . . . Whether an intrusion has in fact resulted in a violation of sovereignty needs to be assessed on a case-by-case basis taking into consideration the nature and character of the intrusion.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,"If a cyber operation results in damage or loss of functionality, then it may be a breach of sovereignty as assessed on a case-by-case basis in accord with Sweden’s position. The alteration of data is presented as an example of a potential breach.",
Sweden,SWE,10(a) Do state sponsored ransomware operations violate the sovereignty of another State?,Silent,,,,,
Sweden,SWE,10(b) Can state cyber operations against data residing in cloud servers outside of a State’s territorial jurisdiction constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Sweden,SWE,10(c) Does the exercise of one State’s law enforcement tools via cyber means in the networks or systems of another State’s territory violate the sovereignty of the latter State?,Silent,,,,,
Sweden,SWE,11. Do State cyber operations that interfere at any level with critical infrastructure in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Sweden,SWE,12. Can State cyber operations that cause significant national economic impacts for another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Sweden,SWE,13. Does cyber espionage by one State in or transiting networks or hardware resident in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Sweden,SWE,13(a). Can gaining unauthorized access to computer systems or data within a State’s sovereign control violate the rules of sovereignty?,Silent,,,,,
Sweden,SWE,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Yes,Whether an intrusion has in fact resulted in a violation of sovereignty needs to be assessed on a case-by-case basis taking into consideration the nature and character of the intrusion.,Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/U5HT-GWEZ,,
Switzerland,CHE,1.  Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,"Sovereignty is a foundational principle of international law...Sovereignty is a binding primary rule of international law. Violations of sovereignty are therefore considered internationally wrongful acts which, if attributable to the state itself, give rise to state responsibility.","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 86-7, §2 (2021).",/static/documents/41_switzerlands-position-paper-on-the-application-of-internatio.pdf,,
Switzerland,CHE,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"It refers to a state's jurisdiction to define, apply and enforce its own legal order, which in principle is limited to its territory … State sovereignty is also applicable to cyberspace... For example, state sovereignty protects information and communication technologies (ICT) infrastructure on a state's territory against unauthorised intrusion or material damage. This includes the computer networks systems and software supported by the ICT infrastructure, regardless of whether the infrastructure is private or public.","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 86-87, §2 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Switzerland,CHE,"2(a). Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"Sovereignty … refers to a state's jurisdiction to define, apply and enforce its own legal order, which in principle is limited to its territory. Conversely, in terms of interstate relations at cybersecurity level, the principle of sovereignty provides wide scope for protection against cyber operations. For example, state sovereignty protects information and communication technologies (ICT) infrastructure on a state's territory against unauthorised intrusion or material damage. This includes the computer networks, systems and software supported by the ICT infrastructure, regardless of whether the infrastructure is private or public.","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 86-7, §2 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Switzerland,CHE,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"Human rights also apply in the digital space and are a key pillar in the international regulatory framework for digitalisation... This also applies to state security activities in cyberspace i.e. part of the digital space. Human rights obligations are equally binding upon states operating in cyberspace as in physical space. This also applies when the cyber operation in question is being carried out extraterritorially, to the extent that the States exercise their sovereign authority in doing so. …A number of specific human rights may be particularly affected by cyber-related activities. An individual's right of access to information, right to privacy, or freedom of expression for example, could be restricted because of cyber operations or other cyber-related measures. A state must be able to justify restricting these or other human rights in cyberspace based on the same rules that apply in physical space. In principle, any act of state interference requires an adequate legal basis. The state must also be able to demonstrate that in the balance of interests its actions are appropriate, necessary and reasonable in order to meet a legitimate objective...Switzerland considers the applicability of human rights to cyberspace to be an unequivocal principle.","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 92, §7 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Switzerland,CHE,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"Sovereignty is a binding primary rule of international law. Violations of sovereignty are therefore considered internationally wrongful acts which, if attributable to the state itself, give rise to state responsibility.","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §2 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Switzerland,CHE,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"Sovereignty is a binding primary rule of international law. Violations of sovereignty are therefore considered internationally wrongful acts which, if attributable to the state itself, give rise to state responsibility. ... Switzerland recognises that defining what constitutes a violation of the principle of sovereignty in cyberspace is particularly challenging and has yet to be clarified conclusively.","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §2 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,"Switzerland’s position acknowledges that what constitutes as a violation of sovereignty is still open to debate, but recognizes violations of sovereignty as internationally wrongful acts in accord with state responsibility. Given the separate treatment of sovereignty and non-intervention, a standalone violation of sovereignty that is not a prohibited intervention still constitutes an internationally wrongful act.",
Switzerland,CHE,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"Switzerland recognises that defining what constitutes a violation of the principle of sovereignty in cyberspace … has yet to be clarified conclusively. It supports considering the following two criteria in such assessments: first, does the incident violate the state's territorial integrity and second, does it constitute interference with or usurpation of an inherently governmental function. A precise definition of these criteria is a question of interpretation and subject to debate. The current debate includes among other aspects i) incidents whereby the functionality of infrastructure or related equipment has been damaged or limited, ii) cases where data has been altered or deleted, interfering with the fulfilment of inherently governmental functions such as providing social services, conducting elections and referendums, or collecting taxes, and iii) situations in which a state has sought to influence, disrupt or delay democratic decision-making processes in another state through the coordinated use of legal and illegal methods in cyberspace e.g. propaganda, disinformation and covert actions by intelligence services.","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §2 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,Criteria: Violation of State’s Territorial Integrity Interference with or Usurpation of an inherently governmental function Switzerland’s position notes that various definitions of this two-part test are still up to debate.,
Switzerland,CHE,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Silent,,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"...the principle of sovereignty provides wide scope for protection against cyber operations.For example, state sovereignty protects information and communication technologies (ICT) infrastructure on a state's territory against unauthorised intrusion or material damage... It supports considering the following two criteria in such assessments: first, does the incident violate the state's territorial integrity and second, does it constitute interference with or usurpation of an inherently governmental function. A precise definition of these criteria is a question of interpretation and subject to debate...Switzerland recognises that defining what constitutes a violation of the principle of sovereignty in cyberspace … has yet to be clarified conclusively.","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §2 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Switzerland,CHE,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Ambiguous,"It supports considering the following two criteria in such assessments: first, does the incident violate the state's territorial integrity and second, does it constitute interference with or usurpation of an inherently governmental function. A precise definition of these criteria is a question of interpretation and subject to debate.","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §2 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,"If a state cyber operation meets the two criteria elicited in the excerpt while causing physical effects in another state’s territory, then such an operation may breach sovereignty, especially if the physical effects cause material damage to ICT infrastructure. The issue lies in the definition and interpretation of these criteria.",
Switzerland,CHE,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,"the principle of sovereignty provides wide scope for protection against cyber operations. For example, state sovereignty protects information and communication technologies (ICT) infrastructure on a state's territory against unauthorised intrusion or material damage.","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §2 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Switzerland,CHE,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,Silent,,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,7. Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,A precise definition of these criteria is a question of interpretation and subject to debate. The current debate includes among other aspects i) incidents whereby the functionality of infrastructure or related equipment has been damaged or limited . . .,"Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §2 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,"Although Switzerland’s position acknowledges that the definition of the criteria is up to debate, the first point of contention is whether effects on functionality constitute a violation of sovereignty.",
Switzerland,CHE,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,7(b) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating significant functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,7(c) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating systemic functionality losses within another state’s territory?,Silent,,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"It supports considering the following two criteria in such assessments: first, does the incident violate the state's territorial integrity and second, does it constitute interference with or usurpation of an inherently governmental function.","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §2 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Switzerland,CHE,9. Must there be proximate causation between the state cyber operation and the effects or interference prohibited by sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,9(a) Must the state cyber operation generate prohibited effects directly?,Silent,,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,9(b) Do prohibited effects indirectly generated by a state cyber operation constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
