Switzerland,CHE,10(b) Can state cyber operations against data residing in cloud servers outside of a State’s territorial jurisdiction constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"One major issue is who has jurisdiction over or access to digital data. In the cyber context, the key question is which states have legitimate control over digital data and are authorised to access that data – which may, depending on the circumstances, be stored on a different territory or may not be localised geographically . . . Conversely, in terms of interstate relations at cybersecurity level, the principle of sovereignty provides wide scope for protection against cyber operations.. . . A precise definition of these criteria is a question of interpretation and subject to debate. The current debate includes among other aspects . . . ii) cases where data has been altered or deleted, interfering with the fulfilment of inherently governmental functions such as providing social services, conducting elections and referendums, or collecting taxes . . .","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §2 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,"Switzerland’s position recognizes that determining control over digital data is difficult, but sovereignty still allows for the protection of such data even extraterritorially. However, Switzerland’s position also acknowledges that the question of how the elements are defined is up to debate, with a point of contention being whether the alteration or deletion of data constitutes a violation of sovereignty.",
Switzerland,CHE,10(c) Does the exercise of one State’s law enforcement tools via cyber means in the networks or systems of another State’s territory violate the sovereignty of the latter State?,Silent,,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,11. Do State cyber operations that interfere at any level with critical infrastructure in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,12. Can State cyber operations that cause significant national economic impacts for another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,13. Does cyber espionage by one State in or transiting networks or hardware resident in another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Silent,,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,13(a). Can gaining unauthorized access to computer systems or data within a State’s sovereign control violate the rules of sovereignty?,Yes,"State sovereignty is also applicable to cyberspace... One major issue is who has jurisdiction over or access to digital data. In the cyber context, the key question is which states have legitimate control over digital data and are authorised to access that data – which may, depending on the circumstances, be stored on a different territory or may not be localised geographically. Conversely, in terms of interstate relations at cybersecurity level, the principle of sovereignty provides wide scope for protection against cyber operations. For example, state sovereignty protects information and communication technologies (ICT) infrastructure on a state's territory against unauthorised intrusion or material damage. This includes the computer networks, systems and software supported by the ICT infrastructure, regardless of whether the infrastructure is private or public","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §2 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Switzerland,CHE,14. Are violations of sovereignty assessed on a case-by-case basis?,Yes,The assessment of an individual case depends on the nature of the cyber incident and its repercussions.,"Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §2 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
Thailand,THA,1.  Is sovereignty a foundational principle of the international legal order?,Yes,¶7. The principle of state sovereignty is a fundamental rule of international law that underpins international relations. It is firmly established in customary international law and enshrined in international legal instruments including the Charter of the United Nations and the ASEAN Charter,"Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 2, ¶7 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,,
Thailand,THA,2. Does sovereignty confer on states authority over cyber infrastructure located in their respective territories and over the actions they take with respect to that infrastructure?,Yes,"¶9. Under international law, every State exercises sovereignty over the ICT infrastructure, persons, and activities within its territory, subject to its international legal obligations. This includes the authority to prescribe and enforce laws and regulations governing cyberspace and to protect critical information infrastructure","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, ¶9 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,,
Thailand,THA,"2(a). Does sovereignty encompass the right of a state to protect cyber infrastructure and safeguard cyber activity that is located in, or takes place on, its territory?",Yes,"¶9. Under international law, every State exercises sovereignty over the ICT infrastructure, persons, and activities within its territory, subject to its international legal obligations. This includes the authority to prescribe and enforce laws and regulations governing cyberspace and to protect critical information infrastructure","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, ¶9 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,,
Thailand,THA,2(b) Is the exercise of such sovereign authority subject to international human rights law and other fundamental freedoms?,Yes,"¶39. Thailand reaffirms that international human rights law (IHRL) applies in cyberspace, as it does in the physical world. States' activities in cyberspace must be conducted in accordance with their international human rights obligations, as expressed in the international human rights treaties to which they are a party, and in customary international law.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 10, ¶39 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,,
Thailand,THA,"3. Is sovereignty a stand-alone, primary rule of international law, the breach of which can incur international responsibility?",Yes,"¶11. Therefore, a cyber activity attributable to a State that causes, or is reasonably expected to cause, harmful effects on ICT systems or infrastructure located within another State’s territory constitutes a violation of that State’s territorial sovereignty. ¶14. Such a breach of the obligation to respect State sovereignty amounts to an internationally wrongful act, even if the cyber operation does not rise to the level of a prohibited intervention or a prohibited use of force under international law.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3-4, ¶11,14 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,,
Thailand,THA,3(a) Can violations of sovereignty not amounting to prohibited interventions constitute internationally wrongful acts?,Yes,"¶14. Such a breach of the obligation to respect State sovereignty amounts to an internationally wrongful act, even if the cyber operation does not rise to the level of a prohibited intervention or a prohibited use of force under international law.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, ¶14 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,,
Thailand,THA,4. Does the State indicate how to identify a violation of any stand-alone sovereignty rule?,Yes,"¶11. Therefore, a cyber activity attributable to a State that causes, or is reasonably expected to cause, harmful effects on ICT systems or infrastructure located within another State’s territory constitutes a violation of that State’s territorial sovereignty. ¶12. State cyber operations targeting another State’s critical infrastructure constitute a violation of sovereignty where they interfere with the State’s sovereign control over the critical infrastructure; cause harm or compromise essential functions of the critical infrastructure.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, ¶11, 12 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,,
Thailand,THA,4(a) Is there a de minimis threshold below which a violation of sovereignty would not occur?,Silent,,,,,
Thailand,THA,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",Silent,,,,,
Thailand,THA,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Ambiguous,"¶11. Therefore, a cyber activity attributable to a State that causes, or is reasonably expected to cause, harmful effects on ICT systems or infrastructure located within another State’s territory constitutes a violation of that State’s territorial sovereignty. ¶12. State cyber operations targeting another State’s critical infrastructure constitute a violation of sovereignty where they interfere with the State’s sovereign control over the critical infrastructure; cause harm or compromise essential functions of the critical infrastructure.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, ¶11, 12 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,,
Thailand,THA,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,Silent,,,,,
Thailand,THA,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",Silent,,,,,
Thailand,THA,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,Silent,,,,,
Thailand,THA,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,Silent,,,,,
Thailand,THA,7 Can state cyber operations that cause a loss to computer functionality in another State’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,Yes,"¶11. Therefore, a cyber activity attributable to a State that causes, or is reasonably expected to cause, harmful effects on ICT systems or infrastructure located within another State’s territory constitutes a violation of that State’s territorial sovereignty. ¶12. State cyber operations targeting another State’s critical infrastructure constitute a violation of sovereignty where they interfere with the State’s sovereign control over the critical infrastructure; cause harm or compromise essential functions of the critical infrastructure.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, ¶11, 12 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,,
Thailand,THA,7(a) Is the rule violated by a cyber operation generating any functionality losses within another state’s territory?,No,¶12. State cyber operations targeting another State’s critical infrastructure constitute a violation of sovereignty where they interfere with the State’s sovereign control over the critical infrastructure, cause harm or compromise essential functions of the critical infrastructure.,"Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 3, ¶12 (2025)",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,Ltd to Critical Infrastructure
