United Kingdom,GBR,"5. Is it a violation of territorial sovereignty for an organ of a State, or others whose conduct is attributable to the State, to conduct non-consensual cyber operations while physically present on another State’s territory against that State or entities or persons on its territory?",No,,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,6. Can state cyber operations that cause physical effects in another state’s territory constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,No,¶19. The United Kingdom does not consider that the general concept of sovereignty by itself provides a sufficient or clear basis for extrapolating a specific rule or additional prohibition for cyber conduct going beyond that of non-intervention referred to above.,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,,
United Kingdom,GBR,6(a) Do any physical effects constitute a violation?,No,"Having identified the importance on the role – the rule on non-intervention, I now turn to the threshold for its application. The fact that behaviour attributed to another state is unwelcome, irresponsible or, indeed, hostile, does not mean that it is also unlawful. A core element of the non-intervention rule is that the offensive behaviour must be coercive . . . In essence, an intervention in the affairs of another state will be unlawful if it is forcible, dictatorial or otherwise coercive, depriving a state of its freedom of control over matters, which it is permitted to decide freely by the principle of state sovereignty.",Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers,https://perma.cc/69JF-VV9Y,,
United Kingdom,GBR,"6(b) Must the effects involve physical damage to computer infrastructure or other tangible things, or injury to persons, to constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?",No,See Question 6(a),Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers,https://perma.cc/69JF-VV9Y,,
United Kingdom,GBR,6(b)(i) Must the effect of a cyber operation to computer infrastructure require repair or replacement of physical components to qualify as damage?,No,See Question 6(a),Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers,https://perma.cc/69JF-VV9Y,,
United Kingdom,GBR,6(c) Do only cyber operations generating significant physical effects within another State’s territory constitute a violation?,No,See Question 6(a),Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers,https://perma.cc/69JF-VV9Y,,
