United Kingdom,GBR,8. Can state cyber operations that interfere with another state’s “inherent governmental functions” constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,No,¶19. The United Kingdom does not consider that the general concept of sovereignty by itself provides a sufficient or clear basis for extrapolating a specific rule or additional prohibition for cyber conduct going beyond that of non-intervention referred to above.,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,"Given that the United Kingdom's position views sovereignty as a general principle rather than a judicially binding rule of international law specifically applicable to cyberspace, cyber operations interfering with another state’s inherent government functions are not considered beyond the scope of non-intervention.",
