United Kingdom,GBR,9(b) Do prohibited effects indirectly generated by a state cyber operation constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,No,¶19. The United Kingdom does not consider that the general concept of sovereignty by itself provides a sufficient or clear basis for extrapolating a specific rule or additional prohibition for cyber conduct going beyond that of non-intervention referred to above.,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,"Given that the United Kingdom's position views sovereignty as a general principle rather than a judicially binding rule of international law specifically applicable to cyberspace, state cyber operations that generate any effects, directly or indirectly, do not explicitly violate any cyber specific rule of sovereignty.",
United Kingdom,GBR,10. Can state cyber operations that deny access to or degrade data in the control of another State constitute a violation of sovereignty as a rule?,No,¶19. The United Kingdom does not consider that the general concept of sovereignty by itself provides a sufficient or clear basis for extrapolating a specific rule or additional prohibition for cyber conduct going beyond that of non-intervention referred to above.,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,"Given that the United Kingdom's position views sovereignty as a general principle rather than a judicially binding rule of international law specifically applicable to cyberspace, cyber operations that deny access to or degrade data in the control of another state may constitute an intervention, but not a violation of any cyber specific rule of sovereignty.",
